1	PNC Music Pavilion	707 Pavilion Blvd	Charlotte	NC	28262	US	35.327991	-80.711207	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:26	2025-07-06 23:22:35.902352	f	\N	\N	PNC Music Pavilion is one of Charlotte's premier outdoor amphitheaters, featuring a distinctive covered pavilion and expansive lawn seating. Located in the University City area, this 19500-capacity venue has been a cornerstone of Charlotte's live music scene since its opening in 1991. The amphitheater combines reserved covered seating with a spacious general admission lawn area, creating a versatile space for both intimate performances and major touring productions.\n\nThe venue has established itself as a crucial stop on major summer tours, hosting some of the biggest names in music across all genres. Its design takes advantage of natural terrain to create excellent sightlines throughout the facility, while modern amenities and regular updates have kept it competitive with newer venues. The pavilion's location, just off Interstate 485, makes it easily accessible while providing sufficient distance from residential areas for sound considerations.	85	["Live Nation venue information","Charlotte Observer archives","Wikipedia","Local news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:41:39.140167	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1991","description":"Opened as one of the largest outdoor music venues in the Carolinas","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Covered pavilion seating","description":"8000 covered seats protected from weather while maintaining outdoor concert experience","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Lawn seating","description":"Spacious lawn area with natural grade providing clear sightlines to stage","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Location accessibility","description":"Situated near I-485 with dedicated access roads to handle large crowds","category":"location"}]	f	\N
11	Veterans United Home Loans Amphitheatre	3550 Cellar Door Way	Virginia Beach	VA	23456	US	36.769109	-76.103678	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:42:14.396873	f	\N	\N	Veterans United Home Loans Amphitheatre (formerly known as Virginia Beach Amphitheater) is one of the largest outdoor music venues in Virginia Beach, Virginia. This modern amphitheater opened in 1996 and features a distinctive covered pavilion with fixed seating plus a spacious lawn area for additional general admission viewing. The venue is situated on approximately 20 acres within the Virginia Beach area.\n\nThe amphitheater has become a cornerstone of the mid-Atlantic summer concert circuit, regularly hosting major touring acts during the outdoor concert season from spring through fall. Its location near the Virginia Beach oceanfront and tourist district makes it a popular destination for both locals and visitors. The venue features modern amenities while maintaining the intimate feeling of an outdoor concert experience.\n\nSince its opening, the amphitheater has undergone several naming rights changes, most recently becoming the Veterans United Home Loans Amphitheatre in 2016. The venue is operated by Live Nation and continues to be the premier outdoor concert facility in the Hampton Roads region.	85	["Live Nation venue information","Local news archives","Virginia Beach tourism resources","Venue website"]	2025-07-06 21:42:14.396534	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1996","description":"Opened as the largest purpose-built outdoor music venue in Virginia Beach area","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Covered Pavilion","description":"Large covered seating area protecting approximately 7500 seats from weather while maintaining outdoor concert atmosphere","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Lawn Seating","description":"Spacious grass hill area accommodating approximately 12500 additional guests for general admission viewing","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Location Advantage","description":"Situated near Virginia Beach tourist district and oceanfront, making it accessible to both locals and visitors","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
30	John Paul Jones Arena	295 Massie Rd	Charlottesville	VA	22903	US	38.045976	-78.509913	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:44:19.33303	f	\N	\N	The John Paul Jones Arena (JPJ) is the largest indoor arena in Virginia, serving as the premier entertainment and sports venue for the University of Virginia and Charlottesville community since its opening in 2006. This state-of-the-art facility spans 265000 square feet and combines modern amenities with excellent acoustics, making it a sought-after stop for major touring artists. The arena was named in honor of John Paul Jones, a generous university benefactor, not the Led Zeppelin bassist of the same name.\n\nThe venue features a distinctive curved roof design and glass-enclosed main entrance, creating an architectural landmark on the university grounds. Its advanced sound system and versatile seating configurations allow it to host everything from intimate theater-style shows to full-arena concerts. The facility has consistently ranked among the top university arenas in the nation for ticket sales and has hosted numerous high-profile artists including Dave Matthews Band, Billy Joel, and Taylor Swift.	85	["University of Virginia official website","John Paul Jones Arena official website","Local news archives","Concert industry databases"]	2025-07-06 21:44:19.332146	completed	[{"event":"Grand Opening Concert","date":"2006-08-01","description":"Facility officially opened with a special concert event","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Dave Matthews Band Performance","date":"2006","description":"One of the first major concerts at the venue, significant as DMB has strong ties to Charlottesville","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Curved Roof Design","description":"Distinctive architectural element that has become a landmark on the UVA campus","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Modern Sound System","description":"State-of-the-art audio equipment designed specifically for the venue's acoustics","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Can be arranged in multiple layouts from theater-style to full arena setup","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
38	Bader Field	545 N Albany Ave	Atlantic City	NJ	08401	US	39.358470	-74.460500	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:44:57.048504	f	\N	\N	Bader Field is a former municipal airport turned event space located in Atlantic City, New Jersey. The 143-acre site holds historical significance as the world's first municipal airport to be designated as such, operating from 1910 to 2006. Since its closure as an airport, the venue has been repurposed for large-scale outdoor entertainment events, particularly music festivals and concerts.\n\nThe expansive coastal location offers unique advantages for major outdoor events, with the Atlantic City skyline serving as a dramatic backdrop. The venue's large flat surface area, originally designed for aircraft, provides excellent sight lines and considerable flexibility for stage and crowd configurations. Due to its proximity to the Atlantic Ocean and bay, the venue benefits from natural cooling breezes during summer events.\n\nBader Field has hosted several significant music festivals, including the Dave Matthews Band Caravan in 2011 and the Phish Festival in 2012. The venue's ability to accommodate large crowds in an outdoor setting, combined with its historic significance and scenic location, has made it a notable destination for major music events in the Northeast region.	85	["Atlantic City Municipal Records","Local News Archives","Concert History Databases","Historical Airport Records"]	2025-07-06 21:44:57.04796	completed	[{"event":"Dave Matthews Band Caravan","date":"2011-06-24","description":"Three-day music festival featuring Dave Matthews Band and multiple other artists","category":"performance"},{"event":"Phish Festival","date":"2012-06-15","description":"Major three-day music festival featuring multiple Phish performances","category":"performance"},{"event":"Airport Closure","date":"2006-09-30","description":"Closure of historic municipal airport, marking transition to event space","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Waterfront Location","description":"Situated between the Atlantic Ocean and bay, offering scenic views and natural cooling","category":"location"},{"feature":"Large Open Space","description":"143-acre former airfield providing flexible configuration options for large events","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Urban Backdrop","description":"Atlantic City skyline provides dramatic setting for performances","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
45	Marion Oliver McCaw Hall	321 Mercer Street	Seattle	WA	98109	US	47.623954	-122.350765	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:45:48.305706	f	\N	\N	Marion Oliver McCaw Hall, located in Seattle Center, is the city's premier performance venue and home to the Pacific Northwest Ballet and Seattle Opera. Originally opened in 1928 as the Civic Auditorium, the building underwent a major $127 million renovation completed in 2003 that transformed it into the modern facility it is today. The renovation maintained the building's historic facade while creating a stunning glass-enclosed lobby and modernizing the performance spaces.\n\nThe hall features a 2900-seat main auditorium with excellent acoustics, state-of-the-art technical capabilities, and superior sightlines. The venue is renowned for its dramatic five-story glass lobby, featuring Bill Fontana's sonic art installation and Lino Tagliapietra's dramatic glass sculptures. The interior combines elegant design with cutting-edge theatrical technology, making it one of the most technically advanced theaters in North America.\n\nBeyond serving as home to Seattle's premier ballet and opera companies, McCaw Hall hosts a wide variety of performances and events throughout the year, from Broadway shows to contemporary dance performances. The venue's excellent acoustics and intimate atmosphere, despite its large capacity, have made it a favorite among performers and audiences alike. Its location within Seattle Center, near the Space Needle, makes it a cultural cornerstone of the city.	90	["Seattle Center website","McCaw Hall official website","Seattle Opera historical records","Pacific Northwest Ballet archives"]	2025-07-06 21:45:48.305354	completed	[{"event":"Seattle World's Fair","date":"1962","description":"Venue served as a key facility during the Century 21 Exposition","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"2003","description":"$127 million renovation transformed the facility into a state-of-the-art performance venue","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Five-story glass lobby","description":"Dramatic entrance featuring Bill Fontana's sonic art installation and Lino Tagliapietra glass sculptures","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Advanced acoustics","description":"State-of-the-art sound design optimized for both opera and orchestral performances","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Lecture Hall","description":"Additional 380-seat Lecture Hall for smaller performances and events","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
117	Tempodrom	Möckernstraße 1	Berlin	BE	10963	DE	52.516670	13.383330	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:52:41.662165	f	\N	\N	The Tempodrom is one of Berlin's most distinctive contemporary entertainment venues, characterized by its striking circus tent-inspired architecture featuring a dramatic pointed roof design. Originally established in 1980 as a temporary tent structure by Irene Moessinger, it evolved from a makeshift cultural space into a permanent modern facility at its current location near Potsdamer Platz in 2001.\n\nThe current building, designed by architects Gerkan, Marg & Partners, maintains the spirit of the original tent through its innovative concrete shell roof structure while providing state-of-the-art facilities. The main hall features a flexible arena-style configuration that can accommodate various event types, from concerts and theatrical performances to corporate events and circus shows.\n\nSince its opening, the Tempodrom has become a vital part of Berlin's cultural landscape, hosting numerous significant performances and events. Its unique architectural design, excellent acoustics, and versatile space configuration have made it a favorite venue for both artists and audiences, continuing its legacy as a symbol of Berlin's dynamic cultural scene.	85	["Wikipedia","Berlin.de","Tempodrom official website","Architecture databases"]	2025-07-06 21:52:41.661579	completed	[{"event":"Opening as temporary tent venue","date":"1980","description":"Founded by Irene Moessinger as an alternative cultural space","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Opening of permanent building","date":"2001","description":"Inauguration of current modern facility designed by Gerkan, Marg \\u0026 Partners","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Tent-inspired architecture","description":"Distinctive concrete shell roof structure reminiscent of circus tent design","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Flexible configuration","description":"Adaptable main arena space suitable for various event types","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Multiple venues","description":"Contains main arena plus smaller halls for diverse programming","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
24	Ameris Bank Amphitheatre	2200 Encore Pkwy	Alpharetta	GA	30009	US	34.054351	-84.306086	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 23:22:35.919204	f	\N	\N	The Verizon Wireless Amphitheatre at Encore Park (now Ameris Bank Amphitheatre) is a major outdoor concert venue located in Alpharetta, Georgia, a northern suburb of Atlanta. Opened in 2008, the venue was developed through a unique partnership between the City of Alpharetta and the Atlanta Symphony Orchestra, making it both a summer home for the symphony and a premier location for popular music concerts.\n\nThe amphitheater features a covered seating pavilion and a spacious lawn area, combining the comfort of reserved seating with the casual atmosphere of lawn seating that has become a hallmark of summer concert experiences. The venue's design incorporates modern amenities while maintaining excellent sight lines and acoustic properties throughout the facility.\n\nAs one of metro Atlanta's largest outdoor music venues, it has hosted numerous prominent artists across multiple genres and serves as an important cultural center for the north Atlanta suburbs. The facility includes premium amenities like VIP boxes and club seating, while maintaining a connection to its symphony orchestra roots through specialized acoustic design elements.	80	["Live Nation venue information","Local news archives","Atlanta Symphony Orchestra historical records","Municipal records"]	2025-07-06 21:43:39.889165	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"2008-05-10","description":"Inaugural season featuring the Atlanta Symphony Orchestra as resident orchestra","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Hybrid Design","description":"Unique design accommodating both symphony orchestra performances and popular music concerts with specialized acoustic considerations","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Premium Seating Options","description":"Features both covered pavilion seating and open lawn spaces, plus VIP boxes and club seating areas","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Location Advantage","description":"Situated in affluent North Atlanta suburbs with easy access from GA-400 and convenient parking","category":"location"}]	f	\N
55	DTE Energy Music Theatre	7774 Sashabaw Road	Ada	MI	48348	US	42.747045	-83.373497	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:46:33.795102	f	\N	\N	I notice there appears to be an error in the provided venue information. DTE Energy Music Theatre is actually located in Clarkston, Michigan (not Ada, MI). The venue at 7774 Sashabaw Road in Clarkston, MI is a major amphitheater formerly known as Pine Knob Music Theatre. Without accurate baseline information about which specific venue to research, I cannot provide a reliable comprehensive history as the location data appears incorrect.	10	[]	2025-07-06 21:46:33.794217	completed	[]	[]	\N	\N
200	Scotiabank Arena	40 Bay Street	Toronto	ON	M5J 2L2	CA	43.657283	-79.383897	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-05-18 05:22:23	2025-07-06 23:22:36.034007	f	\N	\N	The Air Canada Centre (now known as Scotiabank Arena) is a major multi-purpose arena in downtown Toronto that opened in 1999. Located at 40 Bay Street, it serves as the home of the NHL's Toronto Maple Leafs and NBA's Toronto Raptors, while also functioning as one of Canada's premier concert venues. The arena features a distinctive architecture that incorporates the historic Toronto Postal Delivery Building's south façade.\n\nWith a concert capacity of approximately 19800, the venue has hosted many of the world's biggest musical acts and entertainment events. Its downtown location, connected to Toronto's Union Station and PATH system, makes it highly accessible. The arena features state-of-the-art acoustics and multiple configuration options to accommodate different types of performances and events.\n\nAs one of North America's busiest arenas, it consistently ranks among the top venues worldwide for ticket sales. The facility includes modern amenities, luxury suites, and multiple food and beverage options. Its significance in Toronto's entertainment landscape has only grown since its opening, hosting everything from major concert tours to awards shows and special events.	90	["Official Scotiabank Arena website","Wikipedia","Toronto City Archives","MLSE Corporate Records"]	2025-07-06 22:01:23.059562	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1999-02-19","description":"First event held at the arena featuring the Toronto Maple Leafs","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"2016 NBA All-Star Game","date":"2016-02-14","description":"First NBA All-Star Game held outside the United States","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Historic Façade Integration","description":"Incorporates the historic Toronto Postal Delivery Building's south façade into modern design","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Downtown Connection","description":"Direct connection to Union Station and Toronto's PATH underground pedestrian system","category":"location"},{"feature":"Versatile Configuration","description":"Multiple seating configurations available for different types of events","category":"stage"}]	f	\N
75	Nationwide Arena	200 W Nationwide Blvd	Columbus	OH	43215	US	39.968836	-83.006953	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:48:41.65151	f	\N	\N	Nationwide Arena is a multi-purpose indoor arena located in Columbus, Ohio's Arena District. Opened in 2000, it serves as the home of the NHL's Columbus Blue Jackets and is one of the premier entertainment venues in the Midwest. The arena was built as part of a successful effort to bring professional hockey to Columbus and has since become a cornerstone of the city's downtown revival.\n\nThe venue features modern architecture with a distinctive glass-enclosed atrium and a terraced entry plaza. With a concert capacity of approximately 20000, it offers excellent sightlines and state-of-the-art acoustics. The arena's design incorporates numerous premium amenities including luxury suites, club seating, and multiple concession options.\n\nSince its opening, Nationwide Arena has hosted countless major touring acts and special events, establishing itself as Central Ohio's premier large-scale concert venue. Its location in the heart of the Arena District, surrounded by restaurants, bars, and entertainment options, has helped create a vibrant entertainment destination that draws visitors from throughout the region.	90	["Official Nationwide Arena website","Wikipedia","Columbus Business First archives","Arena District website"]	2025-07-06 21:48:41.649788	completed	[{"event":"Arena Opening","date":"2000-09-09","description":"First major event was a WWE wrestling event","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"First Columbus Blue Jackets Game","date":"2000-10-07","description":"First NHL regular season game played in the arena","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Arena District Location","description":"Located in heart of entertainment district with numerous restaurants and bars nearby","category":"location"},{"feature":"Glass Atrium","description":"Distinctive architectural feature providing natural light and dramatic entrance","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Arena can be configured for various types of events from concerts to hockey","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
86	The Pavilion at Montage Mountain	1000 Montage Mountain Rd	Scranton	PA	18507	US	41.352785	-75.658616	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:49:45.131653	f	\N	\N	The Pavilion at Montage Mountain is a scenic outdoor amphitheater nestled in the Pocono Mountains of Scranton, Pennsylvania. The venue features a covered pavilion seating area and expansive lawn seating, offering panoramic views of the surrounding mountain landscape. Originally opened in the 1990s, it has become northeastern Pennsylvania's premier outdoor concert destination.\n\nThe venue is part of the larger Montage Mountain Resort complex, which operates as a ski resort in winter months. Its unique hillside construction provides natural amphitheater-style viewing, with the stage positioned at the base of the mountain. The covered pavilion protects some seating from weather while maintaining open-air concert atmosphere.\n\nKnown for hosting major touring acts during the summer concert season, the venue has been a regular stop for nationally recognized artists and touring festivals. Its location adjacent to Interstate 81 makes it accessible to audiences from multiple metropolitan areas including Philadelphia, New York City, and Syracuse, while maintaining a mountain resort atmosphere.	75	["Live Nation venue listings","Local news archives","Concert industry databases"]	2025-07-06 21:49:45.131128	completed	[{"event":"Warped Tour Regular Stop","date":"2000s-2010s","description":"Served as a regular northeastern venue for the Vans Warped Tour festival","category":"cultural"}]	[{"feature":"Mountain Setting","description":"Natural amphitheater layout built into mountainside providing elevated viewing from lawn seating","category":"location"},{"feature":"Covered Pavilion","description":"Permanent roof structure covering reserved seating area while maintaining open-air atmosphere","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Dual-Season Facility","description":"Part of active ski resort, with concert venue converting to ski slopes during winter months","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
