120	Jahrhunderthalle	Pfaffenwiese 301	Frankfurt am Main	HE	65929	DE	50.099078	8.519066	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:52:55.753568	f	\N	\N	The Jahrhunderthalle (Century Hall) in Frankfurt am Main is a significant multipurpose venue that serves as one of the region's premier entertainment and cultural centers. Originally built in 1963, the distinctive circular building was designed by Friedrich Wilhelm Kraemer and features a striking dome-shaped roof that has become an architectural landmark in Frankfurt's Höchst district.\n\nThe venue has established itself as a versatile space hosting concerts, theatrical performances, corporate events, and trade shows. Its flexible interior configuration allows it to accommodate various event types, from intimate seated concerts to large-scale productions. The hall is particularly noted for its excellent acoustics within the main auditorium.\n\nAs part of the Jahrhunderthalle complex, the venue includes multiple function rooms, modern technical facilities, and extensive parking facilities. Its location in Frankfurt's west end, while somewhat removed from the city center, has made it an important cultural hub for the region, regularly attracting major touring acts and hosting significant cultural events.	75	["Venue website","Frankfurt Tourism Board","Architectural databases","Historical records"]	2025-07-06 21:52:55.752423	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1963","description":"Opening of the venue as a modern multipurpose facility","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Protected Monument Status","date":"1990s","description":"Building received protected status as an important example of post-war architecture","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Dome Architecture","description":"Distinctive circular building with dome-shaped roof design","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Adaptable interior space that can be modified for different event types","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Multiple Function Rooms","description":"Various sized rooms for different event needs","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
131	Jackson Square	700 Decatur St	New Orleans	LA	70116	US	29.957005	-90.062249	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:54:05.929433	f	\N	\N	Jackson Square is not primarily a concert venue, but rather a historic park and open-air public space in the heart of New Orleans' French Quarter. Originally designed in 1721 as Place d'Armes during French colonial times, the square serves as both a National Historic Landmark and the cultural heart of New Orleans. While occasional concerts and performances do take place here, it functions primarily as a public gathering space surrounded by historic buildings including St. Louis Cathedral, the Cabildo, and the Presbytere. The square is named after Andrew Jackson, with his bronze equestrian statue serving as its centerpiece since 1856. Today, Jackson Square hosts street musicians, artists, and performers daily, making it a vibrant outdoor venue for spontaneous musical performances rather than a traditional concert space. The square is particularly known for its role in jazz history, as many early jazz musicians performed here informally, contributing to the development of New Orleans' distinctive musical culture.	90	["National Park Service records","New Orleans Tourism Board","Louisiana State Museum","Historic New Orleans Collection"]	2025-07-06 21:54:05.929042	completed	[{"event":"Louisiana Purchase Transfer Ceremonies","date":"1803","description":"Site of the ceremonial transfer of Louisiana Territory from France to the United States","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Early Jazz Era Performances","date":"1900s","description":"Significant site for early jazz musicians who performed in and around the square","category":"cultural"},{"event":"Installation of Jackson Statue","date":"1856","description":"Installation of the iconic Andrew Jackson equestrian statue","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Open-air setting","description":"Historic public square surrounded by landmark buildings and iron fence","category":"location"},{"feature":"Artist Colony","description":"Home to local artists who display their work on the fence surrounding the square","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Historic Architecture","description":"Surrounded by St. Louis Cathedral, the Cabildo, and the Presbytere","category":"architecture"}]	\N	\N
141	O2 Academy Birmingham	16-18 Horsefair	Birmingham	ENG	B1 1DB	GB	52.474504	-1.899896	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:55:24.268742	f	\N	\N	The O2 Academy Birmingham is a major music venue located in the heart of Birmingham's entertainment district. Originally opened as the Dome Nightclub in the early 1980s, the venue has evolved into one of the city's premier live music destinations. The three-story building features a striking Art Deco-inspired facade and maintains much of its original architectural character despite various renovations.\n\nThe venue became part of the Academy Music Group in 2000 and was rebranded as the Carling Academy Birmingham before adopting its current O2-sponsored identity in 2009. The main room can accommodate up to 3009 people, making it one of the largest indoor music venues in Birmingham. There are also two smaller performance spaces - O2 Academy2 Birmingham (600 capacity) and O2 Academy3 Birmingham (250 capacity).\n\nOver the years, the venue has hosted performances by numerous major artists including Oasis, The Killers, Amy Winehouse, and Arctic Monkeys. Its central location on Bristol Street, combined with its versatile performance spaces and excellent acoustics, has established it as a crucial part of Birmingham's live music infrastructure.	75	["O2 Academy website","Academy Music Group corporate information","Local news archives","Birmingham City Council records"]	2025-07-06 21:55:24.267605	completed	[{"event":"Academy Music Group Acquisition","date":"2000","description":"Transformation from nightclub to dedicated live music venue","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"O2 Sponsorship Deal","date":"2009","description":"Rebranding as part of nationwide O2 Academy venue network","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Multi-room setup","description":"Three separate performance spaces allowing simultaneous events","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Art Deco facade","description":"Notable architectural features preserved from original building","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Central location","description":"Situated in Birmingham city center with excellent transport links","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
60	Toyota Presents the Oakdale Theatre	95 South Turnpike Rd	Wallingford	CT	06492	US	41.448666	-72.846221	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 23:22:35.936329	f	\N	\N	The Toyota Oakdale Theatre (formerly known as Oakdale Musical Theatre) is a significant entertainment venue in Wallingford, Connecticut that has been operating since 1954. Originally beginning as a tent venue in the Oakdale Farm area, it evolved into a theater-in-the-round before being converted into its current form as a modern indoor amphitheater. The venue is notable for combining an intimate theater setting with larger concert capabilities.\n\nThe theater underwent a major renovation in 1996, transforming from its original theater-in-the-round configuration into a state-of-the-art indoor venue with enhanced acoustics and modern amenities. The renovation increased the seating capacity and added the distinctive dome structure that has become an architectural landmark in the region.\n\nToday, the Oakdale Theatre continues to be one of Connecticut's premier live entertainment venues, hosting a diverse range of concerts, Broadway shows, and family entertainment. Under Live Nation's management, it maintains its historical significance while offering modern production capabilities and comfort for contemporary audiences.	80	["Live Nation venue information","Historical newspaper archives","Connecticut tourism resources","Local historical records"]	2025-07-06 21:47:08.543989	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1954","description":"Opened as a tent venue featuring musical theater productions","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"1996","description":"Complete transformation from theater-in-the-round to modern indoor amphitheater","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Dome Structure","description":"Distinctive architectural dome that has become a local landmark","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Hybrid Design","description":"Combines intimate theater setting with larger concert venue capabilities","category":"architecture"}]	f	\N
154	Columbiahalle	Columbiadamm 13-21	Berlin	BE	10965	DE	52.516670	13.383330	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:56:28.306124	f	\N	\N	The Columbiahalle is a significant mid-sized concert venue located in Berlin's Tempelhof district. Originally constructed in 1951 as a cafeteria for the U.S. Air Force at Tempelhof Airport, the building was converted into a music venue in the late 1990s. The venue features a distinctive industrial architectural style typical of its post-war origins, with high ceilings and an open floor plan that creates excellent acoustics for live performances.\n\nSince its conversion to a concert venue, Columbiahalle has established itself as one of Berlin's premier mid-sized venues, hosting a wide range of national and international acts. The venue is particularly known for its versatile main hall that can accommodate both standing concerts and seated events, with a capacity of approximately 3500 people. Its location near the historic Tempelhof Airport gives it a unique cultural context within Berlin's musical landscape.\n\nToday, Columbiahalle continues to be an important part of Berlin's live music scene, regularly hosting rock, pop, electronic, and alternative music performances. The venue's industrial charm, combined with modern sound and lighting systems, creates an authentic Berlin concert experience that appeals to both artists and audiences.	75	["Berlin Tourism Office","Venue websites","Historical archives","Concert databases"]	2025-07-06 21:56:28.30556	completed	[{"event":"Building Construction","date":"1951","description":"Originally built as cafeteria for U.S. Air Force personnel at Tempelhof Airport","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Venue Conversion","date":"late 1990s","description":"Transformed from military cafeteria into concert venue","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Industrial Architecture","description":"Post-war industrial design with high ceilings and open floor plan","category"