61	North Island Credit Union Amphitheatre	2050 Entertainment Cir	Chula Vista	CA	91911	US	32.588081	-117.006309	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 23:22:35.938078	f	\N	\N	The Sleep Train Amphitheatre (now North Island Credit Union Amphitheatre) is a major outdoor concert venue located in Chula Vista, California, approximately 7 miles southeast of downtown San Diego. Opened in 1998, this 20500-capacity amphitheater features a distinctive covered pavilion seating area and sprawling lawn section, making it San Diego County's largest permanent outdoor concert venue. The amphitheater is strategically positioned to take advantage of the region's mild climate, allowing for an extended concert season.\n\nThe venue has undergone several name changes through various corporate sponsorships while maintaining its status as the premier outdoor music destination in the San Diego area. Its design incorporates modern amenities while providing excellent sight lines and acoustic quality throughout the seating areas. The facility includes premium boxes, orchestra seating, and a general admission lawn area that creates a natural amphitheater setting.	85	["Live Nation venue information","San Diego Tourism Authority","Local news archives","Venue historical documentation"]	2025-07-06 21:47:10.526635	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1998","description":"Opened as San Diego County's largest permanent outdoor concert venue","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Climate-optimized design","description":"Located to take advantage of San Diego's mild weather patterns and ocean breezes","category":"location"},{"feature":"Covered pavilion","description":"Large covered seating area protecting audiences from elements while maintaining outdoor concert experience","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Lawn seating","description":"Expansive grass seating area providing flexible capacity and casual viewing options","category":"amenities"}]	f	\N
206	The Park at Harlinsdale Farm	239 Franklin Road	Franklin	TN	37064	US	35.934768	-86.861359	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-09-24 03:20:38	2025-07-06 23:22:36.035811	f	\N	\N	The Park at Harlinsdale Farm is a historic 200-acre former Tennessee Walking Horse farm turned public park in Franklin, Tennessee. Established in 1933 by W.W. Harlin, the farm gained fame as the home of Midnight Sun, a legendary Tennessee Walking Horse stallion. The property was acquired by the City of Franklin in 2004 and has since been transformed into a multi-use public park while preserving its agricultural heritage.\n\nIn recent years, the venue has become known for hosting the Pilgrimage Music & Cultural Festival, an annual two-day music event that began in 2015. The festival utilizes the farm's natural amphitheater setting and historic structures as unique backdrops for multiple performance stages. The pastoral setting and proximity to downtown Franklin make it a distinctive outdoor concert venue that bridges Tennessee's rural heritage with contemporary cultural events.\n\nThe property features the historic main barn, various outbuildings, and rolling pastures that create natural viewing areas for concerts. Its transformation from a working horse farm to a cultural events space represents a successful blend of historic preservation and modern entertainment venue.	85	["City of Franklin website","National Register of Historic Places database","Pilgrimage Music \\u0026 Cultural Festival website","Local historical records"]	2025-07-06 22:54:15.022753	completed	[{"event":"Home of Midnight Sun","date":"1940s-1950s","description":"Famous Tennessee Walking Horse stallion that brought prominence to the farm","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"First Pilgrimage Music \\u0026 Cultural Festival","date":"2015","description":"Inaugural year of major music festival featuring multiple stages","category":"performance"},{"event":"National Register of Historic Places","date":"2006","description":"Listed on National Register of Historic Places for agricultural significance","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Natural Amphitheater Setting","description":"Rolling hills create natural viewing areas for concert audiences","category":"location"},{"feature":"Historic Main Barn","description":"Iconic structure serves as backdrop for performances","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"200-acre Property","description":"Extensive grounds allow for multiple stages and large audience capacity","category":"amenities"}]	f	\N
210	Credit One Stadium	161 Seven Farms Road	Charleston	SC	29492	US	32.861480	-79.901640	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2021-08-03 16:54:32	2025-07-06 22:02:17.713064	f	\N	\N	Credit One Stadium (formerly Volvo Car Stadium and Family Circle Tennis Center) is a premier outdoor tennis and entertainment venue located on Daniel Island in Charleston, South Carolina. Originally built in 2001 to host the Volvo Car Open (formerly Family Circle Cup) tennis tournament, the facility has evolved into a versatile entertainment destination that hosts both sporting events and live concerts. The stadium underwent a significant $40 million renovation in 2021, modernizing its facilities while maintaining its intimate atmosphere and scenic lowcountry setting.\n\nThe venue features a distinctive architectural design that combines modern amenities with Charleston's traditional architectural elements. Its open-air design takes advantage of the coastal breeze, while providing cover for many seats. The stadium's location on Daniel Island offers a unique setting surrounded by maritime forests and tidal creeks, creating an atmospheric backdrop for performances.	85	["Venue official website","Charleston Post and Courier","Daniel Island News","Local news archives"]	2025-07-06 22:02:17.711949	completed	[{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"2021","description":"Underwent $40 million renovation to modernize facilities and expand capacity","category":"architectural"},{"event":"First Concert After Renovation","date":"2022","description":"Reopened as a modernized concert venue following extensive renovations","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Lowcountry Setting","description":"Located on Daniel Island surrounded by maritime forests and tidal creeks","category":"location"},{"feature":"Modern Amenities","description":"Features premium hospitality areas, upgraded concessions, and improved patron amenities following 2021 renovation","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Versatile Configuration","description":"Convertible space that can host both sporting events and concerts with excellent sightlines","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
4	Budweiser Stage	909 Lake Shore Blvd W	Toronto	ON	M6K 3B9	CA	43.629270	-79.415079	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:26	2025-07-06 21:41:41.120663	f	\N	\N	Budweiser Stage (formerly Molson Amphitheatre) is Toronto's premier outdoor amphitheater located on the grounds of Ontario Place along Lake Ontario's waterfront. This open-air venue features a distinctive covered seating area and spacious lawn section, making it a beloved summer concert destination since its opening in 1995. The amphitheater offers stunning views of both Toronto's skyline and Lake Ontario, creating a unique concert atmosphere.\n\nPurpose-built to replace the original Ontario Place Forum, the venue has hosted many of music's biggest names across multiple genres. Its design incorporates modern amenities while maintaining an intimate atmosphere despite its large capacity. The covered pavilion protects seated guests from weather while the gently sloped lawn area provides excellent sightlines for general admission attendees.\n\nAs Toronto's largest outdoor concert venue, it serves as a vital part of the city's summer culture and music scene. The venue operates seasonally from May to October due to Toronto's climate, making it a highly anticipated part of the warm weather entertainment calendar. Its waterfront location and integration with Ontario Place's grounds make it both accessible and scenic for concert-goers.	90	["Wikipedia","Live Nation venue website","Toronto Tourism resources","Historical news articles"]	2025-07-06 21:41:41.119976	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1995","description":"Replaced the original Ontario Place Forum as Toronto's primary outdoor concert venue","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"David Bowie Performance","date":"1995","description":"One of the first major performances at the newly opened venue","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Waterfront Location","description":"Situated on Lake Ontario offering views of both the water and Toronto 