6	Parc Jean-Drapeau	1 Circuit Gilles Villeneuve	Montreal	QC	H3C 1A9	CA	45.498768	-73.522229	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:26	2025-07-06 21:41:57.484568	f	\N	\N	Parc Jean-Drapeau is a sprawling public park and event space located on Saint Helen's Island and Notre Dame Island in Montreal, Quebec. Originally developed for Expo 67, the park has evolved into one of Montreal's premier outdoor concert and festival venues. The park features multiple event spaces, with its main concert area being the striking geometric amphitheater that opened in 2019 after significant renovations.\n\nThe venue is perhaps best known as the home of major Montreal festivals including Osheaga, Heavy Montreal, and ÎleSoniq. Its unique island setting provides stunning views of the Montreal skyline while offering an urban escape surrounded by nature. The amphitheater can accommodate large-scale productions with state-of-the-art sound systems designed to minimize noise pollution to surrounding areas.\n\nIn recent years, the venue has undergone extensive modernization, including the construction of a permanent amphitheater structure in 2019 that replaced the temporary stages previously used for events. This new facility features improved sight lines, enhanced acoustics, and better crowd flow while maintaining the natural park setting that makes the venue unique.	85	["Parc Jean-Drapeau official website","Montreal Tourism Board","Wikipedia","News archives about venue renovations"]	2025-07-06 21:41:57.484219	completed	[{"event":"Expo 67","date":"1967","description":"Site hosted the 1967 International and Universal Exposition, which transformed the islands into a major public space","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"First Osheaga Music Festival","date":"2006","description":"Launch of what would become one of Canada's largest music festivals","category":"cultural"},{"event":"New Amphitheater Opening","date":"2019","description":"Opening of permanent concert facility with improved amenities and capacity","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Island Setting","description":"Located on two islands in the St. Lawrence River with panoramic views of Montreal skyline","category":"location"},{"feature":"Modern Amphitheater","description":"Purpose-built permanent concert facility with enhanced acoustics and sight lines","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Natural Environment","description":"Blend of urban park setting with modern concert facilities","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
7	Wrigley Field	1060 W Addison St	Chicago	IL	60613	US	41.947630	-87.656376	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:26	2025-07-06 21:42:00.160965	f	\N	\N	Wrigley Field is an iconic baseball stadium and concert venue located on Chicago's North Side. Built in 1914, this historic ballpark has become one of the most distinctive concert venues in America, offering performers and fans a unique outdoor music experience within a legendary sporting cathedral. The ivy-covered brick walls, hand-operated scoreboard, and intimate neighborhood setting create an atmosphere unlike any other major concert venue.\n\nWhile primarily known as the home of the Chicago Cubs, Wrigley Field began hosting major concerts in 2005 with Jimmy Buffett's performance. Since then, it has welcomed some of music's biggest names, including Paul McCartney, Billy Joel, Pearl Jam, and Elton John. The venue's limited concert schedule (typically 3-4 shows per summer) makes each performance a special event in Chicago's cultural calendar.\n\nThe stadium's open-air design, coupled with its urban location and historic architecture, creates a one-of-a-kind concert experience that blends intimate sight lines with grand scale. Modern amenities and updates have been carefully integrated while preserving the venue's historic character, making Wrigley Field concerts a bucket-list destination for both artists and fans.	90	["Wikipedia","MLB.com","Chicago Tribune archives","Wrigley Field official website","Live Nation concert records"]	2025-07-06 21:42:00.160565	completed	[{"event":"First Concert at Wrigley Field","date":"2005-09-04","description":"Jimmy Buffett performed the first concert at Wrigley Field, breaking the venue's 91-year music drought","category":"performance"},{"event":"Paul McCartney Concerts","date":"2011-07-31","description":"First Beatles member to perform at the historic venue, selling out two nights","category":"performance"},{"event":"Pearl Jam Live at Wrigley Field","date":"2013-07-19","description":"Legendary rain-delayed concert that became part of Pearl Jam lore","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Ivy-covered outfield walls","description":"Historic ivy-covered brick walls provide a unique backdrop for concerts","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Urban setting","description":"Located in the heart of Chicago's Wrigleyville neighborhood, offering unique cityscape views","category":"location"},{"feature":"Historic manual scoreboard","description":"Last remaining manual scoreboard in Major League Baseball, adding to venue's historic atmosphere","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Rooftop viewing","description":"Surrounding buildings offer additional viewing opportunities for some concerts","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
10	Zilker Park	2100 Barton Springs Rd	Austin	TX	78704	US	30.267767	-97.767905	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:42:13.058004	f	\N	\N	Zilker Park is Austin's most iconic and beloved urban park, spanning 351 acres in the heart of the city along the south bank of Lady Bird Lake. Since its establishment in 1917, it has grown into the city's premier outdoor entertainment venue, hosting major music festivals and cultural events. The park features vast rolling lawns perfect for large-scale concerts and festivals, with the stunning Austin skyline serving as a dramatic backdrop.\n\nThe venue is perhaps best known as the home of the Austin City Limits Music Festival (ACL), which has been held here annually since 2002. The festival has helped establish Zilker Park as one of America's premier outdoor music venues, hosting performances by some of music's biggest names across multiple stages. The park's natural amphitheater-like terrain and spacious grounds can accommodate large crowds while maintaining sight lines and sound quality.\n\nBeyond ACL, Zilker Park hosts numerous other cultural events throughout the year, including the Blues on the Green summer concert series and various community festivals. Its combination of natural beauty, central location, and versatile space makes it a unique venue that epitomizes Austin's outdoor-loving, music-centric culture. The venue continues to play a vital role in Austin's claim as the 'Live Music Capital of the World.'	90	["City of Austin Parks \\u0026 Recreation Department","National Register of Historic Places","Austin City Limits Festival official history","Austin History Center","Local news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:42:13.056902	completed	[{"event":"First Austin City Limits Music Festival","date":"2002","description":"Inaugural ACL Festival established the park as a major festival venue","category":"cultural"},{"event":"Blues on the Green Launch","date":"1990s","description":"Launch of free summer concert series that continues today","category":"cultural"},{"event":"National Register of Historic Places","date":"1997","description":"Park added to the National Register of Historic Places","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Natural Setting","description":"351 acres of open space with natural elevation changes creating amphitheater-like viewing areas","category":"location"},{"feature":"Multiple Stage Locations","description":"Various areas within the park can accommodate different stage setups and crowd sizes","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Downtown Skyline View","description":"Dramatic backdrop of Austin skyline visible during performances","category":"location"},{"feature":"Natural Acoustics","description":"Open space and terrain help with sound distribution for large events","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
9	Gorge Amphitheatre	754 Silica Road	Aberdeen	WA	98848	US	47.100606	-119.993611	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:42:13.172667	f	\N	\N	I notice there appears to be an error in the venue information provided - The Gorge Amphitheatre is actually located in George, Washington (near Quincy, WA), not Aberdeen, WA. The Gorge Amphitheatre is a spectacular 27500-capacity outdoor concert venue situated on a dramatic cliff edge overlooking the Columbia River Gorge. The venue opened in 1985 and has become one of the most scenic and renowned outdoor music venues in North America. The natural amphitheater offers stunning views of the Columbia River, basalt cliffs, and the high desert landscape of central Washington.\n\nThe venue is particularly famous for its breathtaking backdrop and natural acoustics. The sloping lawn seating provides excellent sightlines for audiences, while the stage is positioned to frame sunset views during evening performances. The Gorge has hosted many major touring acts and festivals, including the Sasquatch! Music Festival (2002-2018), and regular performances by Dave Matthews Band, who have recorded live albums at the venue.\n\nCurrently operated by Live Nation, the venue features on-site camping facilities and typically operates during the summer concert season from May through October. Its remote location about 150 miles east of Seattle has made it a destination venue, with many fans traveling significant distances and camping on-site for multi-day events.	90	["Wikipedia","Live Nation venue website","Historical concert databases","Seattle Times archives","Rolling Stone articles"]	2025-07-06 21:42:13.172412	completed	[{"event":"Dave Matthews Band performances","date":"1997-present","description":"Band has performed regularly at the venue since 1997, recording multiple live albums including 'Live at The Gorge'","category":"performance"},{"event":"Sasquatch! Music Festival","date":"2002-2018","description":"Annual music festival that helped establish The Gorge as a major festival venue","category":"cultural"},{"event":"Pearl Jam performance","date":"2005-09-01","description":"Notable performance recorded for 'Live at the Gorge 05/06'","category":"recording"}]	[{"feature":"Natural amphitheater setting","description":"Built into a natural cliff overlooking Columbia River Gorge with panoramic views","category":"location"},{"feature":"Camping facilities","description":"Extensive on-site camping areas supporting multi-day festivals and events","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Natural acoustics","description":"Exceptional sound quality due to natural amphitheater formation and cliff walls","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Sunset views","description":"Stage positioned to incorporate dramatic sunset views over Columbia River Gorge during performances","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
12	Alpine Valley Music Theatre	W2699 County Road D	East Troy	WI	53121	US	42.742095	-88.469645	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:42:26.773979	f	\N	\N	Alpine Valley Music Theatre is one of the largest outdoor amphitheaters in the United States, nestled in the rolling hills of East Troy, Wisconsin. Opened in 1977, this iconic venue features a distinctive covered pavilion and expansive lawn seating spread across its natural amphitheater setting. The venue's scenic rural location, approximately 40 miles southwest of Milwaukee, creates a unique concert experience where music meets nature.\n\nThe amphitheater has played host to numerous legendary performances over its history, becoming particularly famous as a favorite venue of the Grateful Dead and as the site of Stevie Ray Vaughan's final concert before his tragic helicopter crash in 1990. Its massive capacity of approximately 37000 (with 7500 reserved seats and lawn space for about 29500) makes it one of the largest outdoor music venues in the Midwest.\n\nAlpine Valley remains a premier summer concert destination, particularly known for hosting major touring acts and festivals. The venue's natural bowl shape provides excellent sight lines and acoustics, while its rural setting offers a pastoral escape for concert-goers. Despite challenges over the years, including weather-related issues and changing music industry dynamics, it continues to be an important part of the American concert venue landscape.	85	["Wikipedia","Live Nation venue information","Historical news articles","Concert databases"]	2025-07-06 21:42:26.773492	completed	[{"event":"Stevie Ray Vaughan's Final Concert","date":"1990-08-26","description":"Stevie Ray Vaughan performed his final concert here before tragically dying in a helicopter crash leaving the venue","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Grateful Dead Performances","date":"1980s-1990s","description":"Regular performances by the Grateful Dead made this a significant venue in the band's touring history","category":"cultural"}]	[{"feature":"Natural Amphitheater Setting","description":"Built into a natural hillside providing excellent sight lines and acoustic properties","category":"location"},{"feature":"Covered Pavilion","description":"Large covered seating area protecting reserved seats from weather while maintaining outdoor atmosphere","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Rural Location","description":"Scenic setting in Wisconsin countryside provides unique concert experience away from urban areas","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
