26	Olympia Bruno Coquatrix	28 Boulevard des Capucines	Paris	IDF	75009	FR	48.870152	2.328364	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:43:41.821732	f	\N	\N	L'Olympia Bruno Coquatrix is Paris's oldest and most iconic music hall, located in the 9th arrondissement. Founded in 1888, the legendary red-neon façade has become synonymous with Parisian entertainment culture. The venue has played a pivotal role in French music history, hosting countless legendary performances from Édith Piaf to The Beatles.\n\nThe intimate theater setting features distinctive architecture with its classic red velvet interior and renowned acoustics. Under Bruno Coquatrix's management from 1954, L'Olympia became known as the premier venue for launching music careers in France. Its stage has witnessed career-defining moments for artists like Jacques Brel, Charles Aznavour, and Johnny Hallyday.\n\nToday, L'Olympia remains one of Paris's most prestigious performance venues, maintaining its historical charm while hosting contemporary artists. The venue survived multiple threats of demolition, including a notable save in 1993 when it was classified as a historical monument. Its cultural significance extends beyond music, representing a living piece of French entertainment heritage.	90	["Official L'Olympia website","French Ministry of Culture records","Paris tourist authority","Wikipedia","Historical newspaper archives"]	2025-07-06 21:43:41.820527	completed	[{"event":"Édith Piaf performances","date":"1950s","description":"Legendary series of concerts that helped establish L'Olympia as a premier music venue","category":"performance"},{"event":"The Beatles concerts","date":"1964","description":"Historic performances during the height of Beatlemania","category":"performance"},{"event":"Historical Monument Classification","date":"1993","description":"Venue saved from demolition by being classified as a historical monument","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Red neon faça