48	Apollo Theatre	Shaftesbury Avenue	London	ENG	\N	GB	51.657077	-0.042423	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:46:01.633496	f	\N	\N	The Apollo Theatre is a historic West End theatre located on Shaftesbury Avenue in London's City of Westminster. Designed by architect Lewin Sharp, this Grade II listed building opened in 1901 with a distinctive Edwardian facade featuring a mix of Renaissance and Baroque architectural elements. The theatre's ornate interior includes remarkable ceiling paintings by T.E. Colcutt and intricate gilt work throughout its intimate auditorium.\n\nWhile primarily known as a dramatic theatre rather than a concert venue, the Apollo has hosted numerous significant musical performances and productions throughout its history. The venue is particularly notable for its excellent acoustics and intimate atmosphere, making it ideal for both theatrical productions and select musical performances. The theatre features multiple levels including stalls, dress circle, and upper circle, creating different viewing experiences throughout the venue.\n\nToday, the Apollo Theatre continues to be an active part of London's vibrant theatrical scene, operated by Nimax Theatres. The venue experienced a significant event in 2013 when part of its ceiling collapsed, leading to extensive renovations and safety improvements while maintaining its historical character. Despite this incident, the theatre remains a beloved cultural landmark in London's entertainment district.	90	["Historic England","The Theatres Trust","London Theatre Guide","Official venue documentation","Westminster City Council records"]	2025-07-06 21:46:01.632947	completed	[{"event":"Theatre Opening","date":"1901-02-21","description":"Opened with American musical comedy 'The Belle of Bohemia'","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Ceiling Collapse","date":"2013-12-19","description":"Partial collapse of ceiling during performance, leading to major renovation","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Edwardian Architecture","description":"Grade II listed building with Renaissance and Baroque elements","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Intimate Auditorium","description":"Multi-level theatre with excellent sightlines and acoustics","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Ceiling Artwork","description":"Original paintings by T.E. Colcutt with ornate gilt work","category":"architecture"}]	\N	\N
49	Governors Island	Ferry Line Rd	New York	NY	10004	US	40.730600	-73.986600	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:46:04.981042	f	\N	\N	Governors Island is a 172-acre island in New York Harbor that has transformed from a military base into a unique public venue and cultural destination. While not a traditional concert venue, the island hosts various outdoor music events and festivals in multiple locations across its grounds, including the Parade Ground, Nolan Park, and Play Lawn. The venue offers stunning views of Lower Manhattan, the Statue of Liberty, and New York Harbor as a backdrop for performances.\n\nThe island's concert spaces are primarily open-air venues that take advantage of the historic setting and expansive grounds. Events range from small intimate performances to large-scale festivals, with the space being particularly active during the summer months when the island is open to the public. The combination of maritime breezes, historic architecture, and panoramic city views creates a unique concert experience unlike any other in New York City.\n\nSince its transformation into a public space in 2003, Governors Island has become an increasingly popular destination for music events, arts performances, and cultural festivals. The venue is particularly notable for hosting the annual Jazz Age Lawn Party and various contemporary music festivals, making creative use of its historic military buildings and parade grounds as event spaces.	85	["National Park Service records","Governors Island official website","NYC Parks Department","Historical news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:46:04.98037	completed	[{"event":"First public access as event space","date":"2003","description":"Island transferred from federal government to New York state/city control and opened for public events","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Jazz Age Lawn Party establishment","date":"2005","description":"Launch of recurring vintage jazz festival that became a signature cultural event","category":"cultural"}]	[{"feature":"Harbor views","description":"360-degree views of Manhattan skyline, Statue of Liberty, and New York Harbor","category":"location"},{"feature":"Historic setting","description":"Performance spaces incorporate preserved 19th-century military buildings and landscapes","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Multiple venues","description":"Various configurable outdoor spaces for different types of performances and crowd sizes","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Ferry access","description":"Unique arrival experience via ferry from Manhattan or Brooklyn","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
52	Concord Pavilion	2000 Kirker Pass Road	Concord	CA	94521	US	37.960355	-121.939656	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:46:19.263398	f	\N	\N	The Concord Pavilion is a premier outdoor amphitheater nestled in the rolling hills of Concord, California. Designed by renowned architect Frank Gehry and landscape architect Peter Walker, this 12500-seat venue opened in 1975 as a joint project between the City of Concord and local arts supporters. The distinctive architecture features a dramatically curved roof structure that has become an iconic landmark in the East Bay Area.\n\nThe venue has hosted countless legendary performers across multiple genres, from classical concerts with the Concord Symphony to rock legends like The Who, Journey, and The Eagles. Its unique hillside setting provides natural acoustics and excellent sight lines, while offering stunning views of Mount Diablo and the surrounding landscape.\n\nToday, the Concord Pavilion continues to be one of Northern California's most significant outdoor music venues, operating seasonally from spring through fall. The amphitheater combines the intimacy of a smaller venue with large-venue amenities, including spacious lawn seating, modern concessions, and upgraded VIP areas.	85	["Wikipedia","City of Concord Historical Records","Live Nation venue information","SF Chronicle archives"]	2025-07-06 21:46:19.262486	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1975","description":"Designed by Frank Gehry, opened as a joint venture between city and arts supporters","category":"architectural"},{"event":"Jazz Festival Establishment","date":"1969","description":"Concord Jazz Festival began before permanent venue construction, later made this its home","category":"cultural"}]	[{"feature":"Frank Gehry Architecture","description":"Distinctive curved roof design that became a landmark in the region","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Natural Hillside Setting","description":"Built into natural terrain 