51	United Palace Theatre	4140 Broadway	New York	NY	4140 Broadway	US	42.871108	-77.885561	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:46:19.553357	f	\N	\N	The United Palace Theatre is a stunning example of 1930s movie palace architecture, located in Manhattan's Washington Heights neighborhood. Originally opened in 1930 as Loew's 175th Street Theatre, this magnificent venue was designed by noted theater architect Thomas W. Lamb in an eclectic mix of styles dubbed 'Wonder Theatre' architecture, combining elements of Byzantine-Romanesque and Gothic with Indo-Persian influences. The elaborate interior features intricate gold leaf details, ornate woodwork, and a grand foyer that exemplifies the golden age of movie palaces.\n\nIn 1969, the building was purchased by televangelist Reverend Ike's United Christian Evangelistic Association, which preserved its architectural splendor while operating it as both a church and cultural center. Since 2007, the venue has expanded its programming to become a premier performing arts center, hosting concerts, film screenings, and cultural events while maintaining its spiritual role as home to the United Palace House of Inspiration.\n\nToday, the United Palace stands as Manhattan's fourth-largest theater and Washington Heights' premier cultural institution. Its 3400-seat auditorium provides an intimate yet grand setting for performances, while its outstanding acoustics and preserved architectural details make it one of New York City's most distinctive entertainment venues. The theater continues to serve multiple roles as a spiritual center, cultural hub, and architectural landmark.	90	["National Register of Historic Places documentation","United Palace website","NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission records","The New York Times archives"]	2025-07-06 21:46:19.552558	completed	[{"event":"Opening as Loew's Wonder Theatre","date":"1930-02-22","description":"One of five Loew's Wonder Theatres built in New York and New Jersey","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"National Register of Historic Places listing","date":"2016","description":"Recognized for architectural and historical significance","category":"architectural"},{"event":"Bob Dylan performance","date":"2010","description":"Notable concert helping establish venue's modern identity as concert hall","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Wonder Theatre Architecture","description":"Unique blend of Byzantine-Romanesque, Gothic, and Indo-Persian architectural styles","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Original Wurlitzer Organ","description":"Houses one of the original Robert Morton 'Wonder Morton' theater organs","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Grand Foyer","description":"Three-story high foyer with elaborate gold leaf details and ornate decoration","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Original 1930s acoustic design preserved, providing exceptional sound quality","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
