ance spaces including Polo Fields, Music Concourse, and Hellman Hollow","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Historical Context","description":"Surrounded by historical landmarks, museums, and botanical gardens within the park","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
150	Boch Center	270 Tremont Street	Boston	MA	02116	US	42.350474	-71.064228	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.000832	f	\N	\N	The Citi Performing Arts Center, now known as the Boch Center, is a historic performing arts complex in Boston's Theater District. The venue consists of two primary theaters: the Wang Theatre (formerly the Metropolitan Theatre) and the Shubert Theatre. The Wang Theatre, the complex's crown jewel, opened in 1925 and stands as one of the most magnificent examples of theater architecture from the grand movie palace era. With its opulent French Renaissance design, featuring crystal chandeliers, marble columns, and intricate gold leaf detailing, the theater represents the peak of early 20th-century theatrical grandeur.\n\nThe venue has evolved from its origins as a movie palace to become one of Boston's premier performing arts centers, hosting everything from Broadway shows to classical concerts, popular music performances, and comedy acts. The Wang Theatre underwent a major restoration in the 1980s that preserved its historic architectural elements while modernizing its facilities. The complex has played a crucial role in maintaining Boston's cultural heritage and continues to be a vital part of the city's performing arts scene.\n\nUnder various names throughout its history, including the Metropolitan Theatre and the Wang Center for the Performing Arts, the venue has hosted countless legendary performers and productions. Its transformation from a movie palace to a modern performing arts center reflects the changing nature of entertainment in American cities while maintaining its historic character and cultural significance.	90	["Wang Theatre official history","Boston Historical Society","Massachusetts Cultural Council records","Theatre Historical Society of America"]	2025-07-06 21:56:08.325692	completed	[{"event":"Original Opening","date":"1925-10-17","description":"Opened as the Metropolitan Theatre, one of America's grandest movie palaces","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Restoration","date":"1983","description":"Comprehensive restoration funded by Dr. An Wang, leading to venue renaming","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Grand Lobby","description":"Features marble columns, crystal chandeliers, and ornate gold leaf detailing in French Renaissance style","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Historic Interior","description":"One of the best-preserved examples of grand movie palace architecture from the 1920s","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Stage Size","description":"One of the largest stages in New England, capable of accommodating major theatrical productions","category":"stage"}]	f	\N
99	PNC Park	115 Federal St	Pittsburgh	PA	15212	US	40.446967	-80.005825	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:50:58.700655	f	\N	\N	PNC Park is primarily a baseball stadium serving as the home of the Pittsburgh Pirates, but it also functions as an occasional concert venue. Opening in 2001, this modern ballpark features a stunning backdrop of Pittsburgh's downtown skyline and the Roberto Clemente Bridge, making it one of the most picturesque venues in North America. The natural grass field and intimate seating configuration create unique opportunities for concert staging, with the ability to utilize either a field setup or stadium seating arrangement.\n\nThe venue's location along the Allegheny River, combined with its architectural design that incorporates Pittsburgh's steel and bridge heritage, provides concert-goers with a distinctively Pittsburgh experience. The stadium's open design and riverside location can create interesting acoustic properties, while the panoramic city views add a dramatic visual element to evening performances.\n\nWhile primarily known for baseball, PNC Park has hosted several major concerts featuring artists across various genres, though less frequently than dedicated concert venues. Its capacity for concerts can vary significantly based on stage configuration, ranging from approximately 25000 to 40000 attendees.	90	["PNC Park Official Website","Wikipedia","Pittsburgh Pirates Organization","Local News Archives"]	2025-07-06 21:50:58.6998	completed	[{"event":"Jason Aldean Concert","date":"2014-07-26","description":"First major country music concert held at the venue","category":"performance"},{"event":"Billy Joel Concert","date":"2016-07-01","description":"First performance by Billy Joel at the venue, showcasing its potential for major touring acts","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"City Skyline View","description":"Dramatic backdrop of Pittsburgh's downtown skyline and Roberto Clemente Bridge, creating unique concert atmosphere","category":"location"},{"feature":"Riverfront Location","description":"Located along the Allegheny River, providing natural amphitheater-like setting and acoustic properties","category":"location"},{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Ability to configure for both field-level and stadium seating arrangements for concerts","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
102	Save Mart Center	2650 E Shaw Ave	Fresno	CA	93710	US	36.809027	-119.739799	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:51:09.774764	f	\N	\N	The Save Mart Center is a major multi-purpose arena located on the campus of California State University, Fresno. Opened in 2003, this modern 15544-seat facility serves as the home arena for Fresno State Bulldogs basketball teams while also functioning as the Central Valley's premier entertainment venue. The arena features a distinctive architectural design with a curved roof and glass exterior that has become a landmark in Fresno.\n\nThe venue was constructed at a cost of approximately $103 million and represented a significant upgrade to the region's concert and event capabilities when it opened. Its design incorporates modern amenities and excellent sightlines, with a flexible configuration that can accommodate everything from intimate shows to major touring productions.\n\nSince its opening, the Save Mart Center has hosted numerous high-profile concerts, family shows, and sporting events, establishing itself as a vital entertainment hub for California's Central Valley. The arena's location on the Fresno State campus creates a unique connection between the university community and the broader regional entertainment scene.	85	["Fresno State Athletics website","Save Mart Center official website","Local news archives","Wikipedia"]	2025-07-06 21:51:09.774041	completed	[{"event":"Arena Opening","date":"2003-11-27","description":"Official opening of the venue with a men's basketball game between Fresno State and Oregon State","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Modern Arena Design","description":"Features contemporary architecture with curved roof design and glass exterior","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Can be configured for various events from concerts to basketball games with adaptable seating arrangements","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Campus Location","description":"Located on Fresno State campus, creating unique integration with university community","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
101	Beacon Theatre	2124 Broadway	New York	NY	10009	US	40.780473	-73.981170	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:51:10.666604	f	\N	\N	The Beacon Theatre is a historic Neo-Grecian style theater located on Manhattan's Upper West Side. Opened in 1929 as a movie palace and vaudeville house, the venue was designed by architect Walter Ahlschlager with its most distinctive features being its ornate gilded interiors, detailed ceiling murals, and elaborate Greek-inspired decorative elements. The theater was designated a New York City Landmark in 1979 and has become one of New York's most beloved live entertainment venues.\n\nSince its conversion to a dedicated concert venue in the 1970s, the Beacon has hosted countless legendary performances, perhaps most notably serving as home to the Allman Brothers Band's annual spring residencies from 1989-2014. The venue has also welcomed artists ranging from the Rolling Stones and Jerry Garcia to Queen and Leonard Cohen. In 2006, the theater underwent a comprehensive $16 million restoration by Madison Square Garden Entertainment to restore its original architectural grandeur while modernizing its facilities.\n\nToday, the 2894-seat Beacon Theatre remains one of New York City's premier concert venues, prized by performers and audiences alike for its superior acoustics, intimate atmosphere despite its size, and spectacular architectural details. The venue continues to host over 150 events annually, including concerts across all genres, comedy shows, and special events, while maintaining its status as a treasured landmark of New York's cultural landscape.	90	["Wikipedia","NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission","Madison Square Garden Entertainment website","New York Times archives","Billboard archives"]	2025-07-06 21:51:10.666021	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1929-12-24","description":"Opened as a movie palace and vaudeville house","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"NYC Landmark Designation","date":"1979","description":"Designated as an official New York City Landmark","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Allman Brothers Band Residencies","date":"1989","description":"Began annual spring residencies that would continue through 2014","category":"performance"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"2006","description":"$16 million restoration project by Madison Square Garden Entertainment","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Neo-Grecian Architecture","description":"Elaborate Greek-inspired decorative elements, ornate gilded interiors, and detailed ceiling murals","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Renowned for exceptional sound quality and acoustic properties","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Intimate Atmosphere","description":"Despite nearly 3000 seat capacity, maintains an intimate feeling with excellent sightlines","category":"architecture"}]	\N	\N
103	Ak-Chin Pavilion	2121 N 83rd Ave	Phoenix	AZ	85035	US	33.470827	-112.232880	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:51:13.839411	f	\N	\N	The Ak-Chin Pavilion is one of Phoenix's premier outdoor amphitheaters, serving as a major concert venue since its opening in 1990. Located in western Phoenix, this open-air venue features a distinctive covered pavilion section and sprawling lawn seating area, making it particularly suited for large-scale concerts during Phoenix's cooler months.\n\nThe venue has become a staple of Phoenix's live music scene, hosting numerous major touring acts across multiple genres. Its design takes advantage of Arizona's climate while providing essential shade coverage for thousands of seats. The pavilion section offers protected seating while the lawn area allows for casual viewing under the desert sky.\n\nAs one of Live Nation's key amphitheaters in the Southwest, it continues to be an important stop for major tours and has played host to countless memorable performances over its three-decade history. The venue's corporate naming rights have changed several times, reflecting its ongoing commercial significance in the region.	85	["Live Nation venue information","Local Phoenix news archives","Wikipedia","Historical venue documentation"]	2025-07-06 21:51:13.838586	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1990","description":"Opened as the largest outdoor amphitheater in the Phoenix metropolitan area","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Covered Pavilion","description":"Large covered seating area protecting audiences from sun and weather while maintaining open-air atmosphere","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Lawn Seating","description":"Expansive grass seating area allowing for casual viewing and larger capacity","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Desert Setting","description":"Located in the Sonoran Desert, offering unique atmosphere for outdoor concerts","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
106	Red Rocks Amphitheatre	18300 W Alameda Pkwy	Morrison	CO	80465	US	39.667694	-105.208936	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:51:46.124345	f	\N	\N	Red Rocks Amphitheatre is a world-renowned open-air concert venue carved into massive red sandstone formations in Morrison, Colorado. The natural amphitheater, situated at 6450 feet above sea level, features two 300-foot monolithic slabs of red sandstone (Ship Rock and Creation Rock) that perfectly frame the stage and provide remarkable acoustic properties. The venue seamlessly blends natural geology with architectural design, creating an unparalleled concert setting that has hosted performances since 1906.\n\nThe venue was officially opened as a concert venue in 1941 after extensive development by the Civilian Conservation Corps. Since then, Red Rocks has established itself as one of the most prestigious and unique performance venues in the world, hosting legendary shows including The Beatles (1964), Jimi Hendrix, The Grateful Dead, and U2's famous 1983 concert filmed for 'Under a Blood Red Sky'. The natural acoustics, stunning views of Denver, and the ethereal atmosphere created by the red rocks have made it a bucket-list destination for both performers and music fans.\n\nToday, Red Rocks continues to be owned and operated by the City and County of Denver, hosting over 150 concerts and events annually. The venue has expanded beyond just music to include film screenings, yoga sessions, and graduation ceremonies. It's also home to the Colorado Music Hall of Fame and features the Red Rocks Visitor Center, which showcases the venue's geological and musical history. The amphitheater has been recognized as a National Historic Landmark and consistently ranks as one of the world's best outdoor music venues.	95	["National Park Service records","Denver Mountain Parks documentation","Red Rocks official website","Wikipedia","Colorado Music Hall of Fame archives","Historical news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:51:46.123839	completed	[{"event":"The Beatles Performance","date":"1964-08-26","description":"Only concert The Beatles ever canceled due to rain, though they still visited the venue","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"U2 'Under a Blood Red Sky' Recording","date":"1983-06-05","description":"Iconic live concert film and EP recording that helped establish Red Rocks' international reputation","category":"recording"},{"event":"Civilian Conservation Corps Construction","date":"1936","description":"Major development of the venue by the CCC during the Great Depression","category":"architectural"},{"event":"National Historic Landmark Designation","date":"2003","description":"Venue recognized for its cultural and historical significance","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Natural Acoustic Rock Formation","description":"Two 300-foot sandstone monoliths (Creation Rock and Ship Rock) provide natural acoustic enhancement","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Geological Setting","description":"Venue is set within ancient red sandstone formations dating back 250 million years","category":"location"},{"feature":"Elevation","description":"Located at 6450 feet above sea level with views of Denver metropolitan area","category":"location"},{"feature":"Stage Design","description":"Perfectly angled natural amphitheater with stage roof designed to deflect wind","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
155	OVO Arena Wembley	Arena Square, Engineers Way	London	ENG	HA9 0AA	GB	51.558277	-0.281430	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.006259	f	\N	\N	Wembley Arena, now officially known as OVO Arena Wembley, is one of London's most iconic indoor entertainment venues. Originally built for the 1934 British Empire Games as a swimming pool, it was converted to an indoor arena in 1934. The Grade II listed building features a distinctive architectural style that combines art deco elements with modernist design principles.\n\nThe venue has played a pivotal role in British entertainment history, hosting countless legendary performances from artists like The Beatles, David Bowie, ABBA, and Queen. Its unique design, including the original pool area that was covered to create the current arena floor, gives it exceptional acoustics that have made it a preferred venue for both live performances and recordings.\n\nToday, the 12500-capacity arena continues to be a major destination for international tours, sporting events, and entertainment spectacles. Its location within the broader Wembley complex, alongside Wembley Stadium, makes it part of one of the world's most significant entertainment districts.	90	["Official OVO Arena Wembley website","Historic England listings","Wikipedia","British Listed Buildings database"]	2025-07-06 21:56:50.514213	completed	[{"event":"British Empire Games","date":"1934","description":"Venue originally built to host the swimming events for the 1934 British Empire Games","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"The Beatles performances","date":"1963-1964","description":"Multiple historic performances during the height of Beatlemania","category":"performance"},{"event":"ABBA concert recordings","date":"1979","description":"Recording of live material for 'ABBA in Concert'","category":"recording"}]	[{"feature":"Original pool architecture","description":"The building still contains the original swimming pool beneath the arena