125	Vanderbilt Stadium	Jess Neely Dr	Nashville	TN	37203	US	36.141530	-86.808369	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:53:40.077882	f	\N	\N	Vanderbilt Stadium, also known as Dudley Field, is the primary athletics stadium at Vanderbilt University in Nashville, Tennessee. Originally opened in 1922, it holds the distinction of being the first stadium in the Southern United States to be erected exclusively for college football. While primarily serving as the home field for Vanderbilt Commodores football, the stadium has occasionally hosted major concerts and other events.\n\nThe stadium has undergone several renovations throughout its history, with significant updates in 1981 and 2012 improving its facilities while maintaining its historic character. Located in the heart of Nashville's West End neighborhood, the venue is notable for its horseshoe-shaped design and intimate atmosphere despite its substantial capacity.\n\nThough not primarily known as a concert venue, Vanderbilt Stadium has hosted several significant musical events when larger capacity has been needed in Nashville, particularly for major touring acts that exceed the capacity of traditional music venues in the area.	75	["Vanderbilt University Athletics website","Wikipedia","Nashville historical records","College football historical databases"]	2025-07-06 21:53:40.076675	completed	[{"event":"Stadium Opening","date":"1922","description":"First stadium in the Southern United States built exclusively for college football","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"1981","description":"Significant modernization and expansion of facilities","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Horseshoe Design","description":"Classic horseshoe-shaped stadium layout typical of early 20th century college venues","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Natural Grass Field","description":"Features a natural grass playing surface","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Urban Locati