129	KeyBank Center	1 Seymour H Knox III Plaza	Buffalo	NY	14203	US	42.875005	-78.876412	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 23:22:35.989997	f	\N	\N	The First Niagara Center, now known as KeyBank Center, is a major multi-purpose arena located in downtown Buffalo, New York. Opening in 1996, this modern facility serves as both the home of the NHL's Buffalo Sabres and a premier concert venue for Western New York. The arena features a distinctive design that incorporates elements of Buffalo's industrial heritage while offering modern amenities and excellent sightlines.\n\nThe venue has hosted numerous major touring acts and special events throughout its history, with a capacity of approximately 19070 for concerts. Its downtown location along Buffalo's waterfront makes it easily accessible and provides an iconic backdrop for events. The arena features state-of-the-art sound systems and multiple configuration options to accommodate different types of performances.\n\nAs one of the largest indoor venues in upstate New York, it plays a vital role in Buffalo's entertainment and cultural landscape, drawing major tours that might otherwise skip the region. The facility has undergone several renovations and updates over the years to maintain modern standards while preserving its core character as Buffalo's premier arena venue.	85	["Wikipedia","Buffalo News archives","Venue website","Concert industry databases"]	2025-07-06 21:53:54.13732	completed	[{"event":"Arena Opening","date":"1996","description":"Opened as Buffalo's premier indoor arena venue, replacing the Buffalo Memorial Auditorium","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Downtown Waterfront Location","description":"Located in Buffalo's downtown waterfront district, offering convenient access and urban views","category":"location"},{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Multiple seating configurations available for different types of events","category":"stage"}]	f	\N
128	Rogers Arena	800 Griffiths Way	Vancouver	BC	V6B 6G1	CA	49.277742	-123.108877	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:53:52.345558	f	\N	\N	Rogers Arena is a multi-purpose indoor venue located in downtown Vancouver, British Columbia, serving as both the home of the NHL's Vancouver Canucks and one of Western Canada's premier concert venues. Opened in 1995, the arena was built to replace the aging Pacific Coliseum and features modern architecture with excellent sightlines and acoustics designed for both sports and entertainment events.\n\nThe venue has hosted numerous significant concerts and events, including being a key venue for the 2010 Winter Olympics. Its downtown location, connected to public transit via Stadium-Chinatown SkyTrain station, makes it highly accessible. The arena features a distinctive curved roof design and glass facades that have become an iconic part of Vancouver's skyline.\n\nWith state-of-the-art sound systems and versatile staging capabilities, Rogers Arena regularly attracts major touring artists and has been a stop for nearly every major North American arena tour. The venue's intimate feel despite its large capacity, combined with its modern amenities and central location, has established it as the region's leading indoor concert venue.	90	["Official Rogers Arena website","Wikipedia","Vancouver Tourism Board","NHL.com venue information","Historical news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:53:52.344237	completed	[{"event":"2010 Winter Olympics","date":"2010-02","description":"Served as main ice hockey venue during Vancouver Winter Olympics","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Opening Night","date":"1995-09-19","description":"Bryan Adams performed first concert at the venue","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Center-hung scoreboard","description":"One of the largest and most advanced display systems in North America","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"360-degree concourse","description":"Continuous concourse allowing full circuit of the arena","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Downtown location","description":"Direct connection to public transit and downtown amenities","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
130	3Arena	N Wall Quay	Dublin	L	\N	IE	53.348091	-6.239544	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:54:02.818692	f	\N	\N	The 3Arena (formerly The O2) is Ireland's largest indoor entertainment venue, situated prominently along Dublin's North Wall Quay in the Dublin Docklands. This state-of-the-art amphitheatre-style venue was designed by world-renowned architectural firm HOK Sport and opened in 2008. The distinctive building features a tilted cylindrical shape with a glass-fronted atrium, making it an architectural landmark on Dublin's skyline. The venue was built on the site of the former Point Theatre, maintaining Dublin's entertainment legacy at this location while dramatically modernizing the concert experience. The arena's unique design provides excellent sightlines from all seats, with its horseshoe-shaped seating arrangement ensuring no seat is more than 60 meters from the stage. 3Arena has established itself as Ireland's premier indoor concert venue, hosting many of the world's biggest musical acts and entertainment events. The venue's location on the River Liffey, combined with its modern amenities and superior acoustics, has made it a crucial stop on major international tours. From intimate theatrical configurations to full-capacity concerts, the venue's versatile design allows it to accommodate a wide range of entertainment events while maintaining optimal viewing and listening experiences for audiences.	90	["Official 3Arena website","Wikipedia","Dublin City Council records","Irish Times archives"]	2025-07-06 21:54:02.81839	completed	[{"event":"U2 'Point Depot' performances","date":"1989-12-31","description":"U2 performed a series of memorable hometown shows during their Lovetown Tour","category":"performance"},{"event":"Venue Reopening as The O2","date":"2008-12-16","description":"Reopened after major renovation with Kings of Leon performance","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Amphitheatre design","description":"Horsesh