137	Le Zénith	211 Avenue Jean Jaurès	Paris	IDF	\N	FR	48.690240	2.175993	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:54:43.506427	f	\N	\N	Le Zénith is a pioneering modern concert venue in Paris's 19th arrondissement, opened in 1984 as the first in what would become France's network of Zénith concert halls. Designed by architects Philippe Chaix and Jean-Paul Morel, the venue was conceived as part of Jack Lang's initiative to create modern performance spaces specifically adapted for rock and contemporary music concerts. The distinctive industrial-style architecture features a striking angular metallic exterior and flexible interior space.\n\nThe venue has a unique modular design that allows it to accommodate between 3000 and 6400 spectators, with excellent sight lines and acoustics specifically engineered for amplified music. Its innovative design became so successful that it spawned the 'Zénith concept,' which was replicated across France, with similar venues built in other major cities.\n\nOver its nearly four decades of operation, Le Zénith Paris has hosted countless legendary performances from both French and international artists, establishing itself as one of France's premier mid-sized concert venues. It continues to be a vital part of Paris's cultural landscape, hosting approximately 150 events annually while maintaining its original mission of providing an optimal space for contemporary music performances.	85	["Wikipedia","Official Zénith website","French Ministry of Culture documentation","Concert industry databases"]	2025-07-06 21:54:43.506169	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1984","description":"First purpose-built modern concert venue in France specifically designed for rock and contemporary music","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Zénith Concept Creation","date":"1984","description":"Established the template for a nationwide network of similar venues across France","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Modular Interior","description":"Flexible configuration allowing for different capacity arrangements between 3000 and 6400 people","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Purpose-Built Acoustics","description":"Specifically designed for amplified music performances with optimal sound quality","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Industrial Design","description":"Distinctive metallic exterior architecture that became a template for other Zénith venues","category":"architecture"}]	\N	\N
140	Dick's Sporting Goods Park	6000 Victory Way	Commerce City	CO	80022	US	39.806332	-104.892140	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:55:01.603088	f	\N	\N	Dick's Sporting Goods Park is a multi-purpose stadium located in Commerce City, Colorado, primarily serving as the home of Major League Soccer's Colorado Rapids. Opened in 2007, this 917-acre complex includes the main stadium and 24 surrounding full-sized soccer fields, making it one of the largest professional soccer complexes in North America. While primarily a soccer venue, it has become notable in the concert world particularly as the home of Phish's annual Labor Day weekend concerts since 2011, creating a cultural phenomenon among fans who refer to these events simply as 'Dick's.' The stadium features a distinctive design with swooping roof structures that protect much of the seating while leaving the field open to Colorado's dramatic mountain views. The venue's location in Commerce City provides a unique setting with the Rocky Mountains as a backdrop, particularly striking during sunset concerts. When configured for concerts, the stadium offers both field and stadium seating options.	90	["Colorado Rapids official website","Wikipedia","Denver Post archives","Commerce City municipal records"]	2025-07-06 21:55:01.602809	completed	[{"event":"First Phish Labor Day Run","date":"2011-09","description":"Began annual tradition of Phish performing three-night stands over Labor Day weekend, which has become a major cultural event for fans","category":"cultural"},{"event":"Opening of Stadium","date":"2007-04-07","description":"Grand opening featuring Colorado Rapids vs DC United","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Mountain Views","description":"Offers panoramic views of the Rocky Mountains, particularly striking during sunset concerts","category":"location"},{"feature":"Complex Size","description":"917-acre complex with 24 full-sized surrounding fields, providing unique festival potential","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Natural Grass Field","description":"Kentucky Bluegrass playing surface that can be configured for concert settings","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
138	Hollywood Casino Amphitheatre	19100 Ridgeland Ave	Tinley Park	IL	60477	US	41.544431	-87.778399	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:55:02.12924	f	\N	\N	The Hollywood Casino Amphitheatre in Tinley Park, Illinois is one of the Chicago area's premier outdoor concert venues. Originally opened in 1990 as the World Music Theatre, this large-scale amphitheater features a covered pavilion and expansive lawn seating area. The venue has become a cornerstone of Chicago's summer concert season, hosting major touring acts across multiple genres.\n\nWith a total capacity of approximately 28000 (11000 reserved seats and 17000 lawn spots), the amphitheater is designed to accommodate large-scale productions while maintaining good sightlines throughout. The venue features modern amenities including multiple concession areas, VIP boxes, and a sophisticated sound system optimized for outdoor performances.\n\nOver its three-decade history, the venue has operated under several names due to corporate sponsorship changes, but has maintained its position as a vital part of the Chicago area's live music infrastructure. Its location in Chicago's south suburbs makes it accessible to both city and suburban audiences while providing ample parking and space for the large crowds it regularly attracts.	85	["Wikipedia","Live Nation venue information","Chicago Tribune archives","Local news sources"]	2025-07-06 21:55:02.128662	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1990","description":"Opened as one of the largest outdoor music venues in the Chicago metropolitan area","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Covered Pavilion","description":"Large covered seating area protecting audiences from weather while maintaining outdoor concert experience","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Expansive Lawn Seating","description":"Large grass seating area with natural slope providing good stage visibility","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Location Accessibility","description":"Strategic location in Chicago's south suburbs with extensive parking facilities and multiple access routes","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
181	South by Southwest	\N	Austin	TX	78701	US	30.270000	-97.740000	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.02254	f	\N	\N	SXSW (South by Southwest) is not a traditional concert venue but rather a major annual conference and festival that takes place across multiple venues throughout Austin, Texas. Launched in 1987, SXSW has grown to become one of the largest and most influential convergences of music, film, and interactive media in the world. The festival transforms Austin's downtown area into a massive, multi-venue event space each March, utilizing numerous theaters, clubs, parks, and other locations.\n\nThe music portion of SXSW features thousands of performers across hundreds of stages throughout the city, ranging from intimate club shows to major outdoor concerts. Notable venues include Stubb's BBQ, The Mohawk, Empire Control Room, and various temporary stages erected specifically for the festival. The conference and festival has played a crucial role in launching countless music careers and has become a vital platform for discovering new talent.\n\nWhat makes SXSW unique is its decentralized nature and its ability to integrate seamlessly into Austin's existing entertainment infrastructure while creating temporary performance spaces that accommodate the massive influx of artists and attendees. The festival has become synonymous with Austin's identity as the "Live Music Capital of the World" and serves as a major economic and cultural driver for the city.	90	["SXSW official website","Wikipedia","Austin Tourism Board","Historical news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:59:11.453865	completed	[{"event":"First SXSW Festival","date":"1987","description":"Inaugural festival featuring 700 attendees, far exceeding the expected 150","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Twitter Launch","date":"2007","description":"Twitter gained significant early traction after showcasing at SXSW Interactive","category":"cultural"},{"event":"First Virtual SXSW","date":"2020","description":"Festival moved online due to COVID-19 pandemic","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Multi-venue format","description":"Festival takes place across numerous venues throughout Austin rather than at a single location","category":"location"},{"feature":"Convergence of industries","description":"Unique combination of music, film, interactive media, and technology conferences and festivals","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Downtown integration","description":"Festival utilizes and transforms existing city infrastructure and venues","category":"location"}]	f	\N
142	Germain Amphitheatre	2200 Polaris Parkway	Columbus	OH	43240	US	40.148973	-82.958412	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:55:19.545155	f	\N	\N	The Germain Amphitheatre was an outdoor concert venue located in Columbus, Ohio that operated from 1994 to 2007. Originally opened as Polaris Amphitheater, it was a major stop for touring acts in central Ohio during its 13-year run. The 20000-capacity venue featured a covered pavilion section and lawn seating, typical of modern amphitheaters developed in the 1990s.\n\nThe venue was located near the Polaris Fashion Place mall and was operated by Live Nation Entertainment for most of its existence. After being renamed to Germain Amphitheatre in 2003 due to a naming rights agreement with Germain Motor Company, it continued hosting major touring acts until closing after the 2007 concert season. The property was later demolished and redeveloped.	75	["Local news archives","Concert industry databases","Historical aerial photographs"]	2025-07-06 21:55:19.544827	completed	[{"event":"Venue Closure","date":"2007","description":"Venue closed after 2007 concert season due to competition from other venues and changing concert industry dynamics","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Combined Seating Options","description":"Featured both covered pavilion seating and open lawn areas typical of 1990s amphitheater design","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Strategic Location","description":"Located near Polaris Fashion Place mall in growing northern Columbus suburb area","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
143	Piazza Napoleone	Piazza Napoleone	Lucca	52	55100	IT	43.841429	10.502531	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 21:55:23.597869	f	\N	\N	Piazza Napoleone, also known as Piazza Grande, is the main square in the historic center of Lucca, Italy. This expansive public space serves as a premiere outdoor concert venue, particularly during the Lucca Summer Festival, which has been held here since 1998. The square is surrounded by magnificent baroque and neoclassical architecture, including the Palazzo Ducale (now the seat of the Province of Lucca) and features a central monument to Maria Luisa di Borbone.\n\nThe piazza's history dates back to the early 16th century when it was created as part of urban renewal efforts by Paolo Guinigi. It gained its current name and appearance in the early 19th century during Napoleon's sister Elisa Bonaparte Baciocchi's rule of Lucca. The square underwent significant renovations in the 1800s, establishing its current architectural character.\n\nAs a concert venue, Piazza Napoleone offers a unique blend of historical atmosphere and modern entertainment capabilities. The square's open-air setting, combined with the surrounding historic buildings, creates an extraordinary backdrop for performances. The venue has hosted numerous international artists including Bob Dylan, Elton John, David Bowie, and Roger Waters, making it a significant location in Italy's contemporary music scene.	85	["Comune di Lucca official website","Lucca Summer Festival archives","Italian historical archives","Cultural heritage documentation"]	2025-07-06 21:55:23.596714	completed	[{"event":"Lucca Summer Festival Inauguration","date":"1998","description":"First edition of the now-annual summer music festival","category":"cultural"},{"event":"Creation of Square","date":"1806","description":"Renovation and renaming under Elisa Bonaparte Baciocchi's rule","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Historic Setting","description":"Surrounded by baroque and neoclassical architecture including the Palazzo Ducale","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Open Air Venue","description":"Large open square allowing for flexible concert configurations with historic backdrop","category":"location"},{"feature":"Central Monument","description":"Features prominent statue of Maria Luisa di Borbone","category":"architecture"}]	\N	\N
203	LeBreton Flats Park	Sir John A. Macdonald Parkway	Ottawa	ON	K1A 0M8	CA	45.396657	-75.762078	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-07-12 15:24:42	2025-07-06 22:01:33.26352	f	\N	\N	LeBreton Flats Park is an outdoor festival grounds located along the Ottawa River in Ottawa, Ontario. The site is part of the larger LeBreton Flats area, a historic district that has been redeveloped for public use after its industrial past. The venue primarily serves as the main festival grounds for major Ottawa music events, most notably the annual Bluesfest music festival which has been held there since 2007.\n\nThe park offers a unique concert setting with the Ottawa River and Parliament Hill as dramatic backdrops. Its open-air layout allows for flexible configurations to accommodate various event sizes, from intimate shows to major festivals drawing tens of thousands of attendees. The site features large open spaces that can accommodate multiple stages and festival infrastructure.\n\nAs part of the National Capital Commission's holdings, LeBreton Flats Park represents an important piece of Ottawa's cultural infrastructure, providing a centrally-located venue for major outdoor entertainment events. The site continues to evolve with ongoing development plans for the broader LeBreton Flats area, while maintaining its role as a premier outdoor concert venue in Canada's capital.	75	["National Capital Commission website","Ottawa Tourism","Bluesfest historical records","Local news archives"]	2025-07-06 22:01:33.262988	completed	[{"event":"RBC Ottawa Bluesfest relocation","date":"2007","description":"Venue became the new home of Ottawa's largest music festival, marking a major shift in the city's cultural landscape","category":"cultural"},{"event":"KISS performance at Bluesfest","date":"2009-07-15","description":"One of the largest single performances held at the venue","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Waterfront Location","description":"Situated along the Ottawa River with views of Parliament Hill","category":"location"},{"feature":"Flexible Space","description":"Open grounds allow for multiple stage configurations and festival layouts","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Urban Accessibility","description":"Central location with access to public transit and downtown Ottawa","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
147	ExtraMile Arena	1401 Bronco Lane	Boise	ID	83725	US	43.603640	-116.201300	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:35.999014	f	\N	\N	The Taco Bell Arena, now known as ExtraMile Arena, is a multi-purpose indoor venue located on the campus of Boise State University in Boise, Idaho. Originally opened in 1982 as the BSU Pavilion, this 12380-seat facility has served as the primary indoor sports and entertainment venue for the Boise region for over four decades. The arena has been home to Boise State Broncos basketball and has hosted numerous major concerts, sporting events, and community gatherings.\n\nThe venue features a distinctive octagonal design that has become a recognizable landmark on the BSU campus. Its versatile configuration allows it to accommodate various types of events, from intimate theater settings to full-arena concerts and sporting events. The facility underwent significant renovations in the early 2000s to modernize its amenities and improve the guest experience.\n\nAs the largest indoor arena in the Treasure Valley region, it plays a crucial role in bringing major touring acts and events to Idaho's capital city. The venue's location on the university campus, combined with its proximity to downtown Boise, makes it easily accessible while providing ample parking and modern amenities for concertgoers and sports fans alike.	85	["Boise State University website","ExtraMile Arena official website","Local news archives","Wikipedia"]	2025-07-06 21:55:43.723648	completed	[{"event":"Arena Opening","date":"1982","description":"Facility opens as the largest indoor venue in the Treasure Valley region","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"NCAA Tournament Games","date":"2001","description":"Hosted first and second round games of the NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Octagonal Design","description":"Distinctive eight-sided architectural design that has become a campus landmark","categor