146	Bryce Jordan Center	127 Bryce Jordan Center	University Park	PA	16802	US	40.808833	-77.855740	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:55:41.805554	f	\N	\N	The Bryce Jordan Center (BJC) is a multi-purpose arena located on the University Park campus of Pennsylvania State University. Opened in 1996, this modern facility serves as Central Pennsylvania's premier entertainment and sports complex. The arena features a distinctive curved roof design and expansive glass facade that has become an architectural landmark on campus. Named after former Penn State University President Bryce Jordan, the venue hosts major concerts, family shows, Penn State basketball games, and other sporting events. The facility spans approximately 366000 square feet across multiple levels and features a sophisticated sound system designed specifically for both sporting events and concert performances. The center's location at the heart of Penn State's campus makes it a cultural hub for both the university community and broader Central Pennsylvania region. As one of the largest indoor venues in central Pennsylvania, it has hosted numerous high-profile artists including Bruce Springsteen, Elton John, Billy Joel, and Taylor Swift.	90	["Penn State University website","Bryce Jordan Center official website","Wikipedia","Local news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:55:41.805183	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1996-01-06","description":"Official opening of the facility with Penn State men's basketball game","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"THON","date":"1996","description":"Began hosting Penn State's annual THON dance marathon, one of the largest student-run philanthropy events in the world","category":"cultural"}]	[{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Arena can be configured for multiple types of events including concerts, basketball, wrestling, and other entertainment events","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Modern Design","description":"Features a distinctive curved roof design and glass facade that has become a campus landmark","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Location","description":"Situated on Penn State's University Park campus with easy access to parking and campus facilities","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
