149	Joe Louis Arena	19 Steve Yzerman Drive	Detroit	MI	48226	US	42.328780	-83.044920	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:56:03.556152	f	\N	\N	Joe Louis Arena was a legendary indoor arena that served as Detroit's premier entertainment venue from 1979 to 2017. Named after boxing great Joe Louis, the 19275-seat venue was primarily known as the home of the Detroit Red Wings NHL team but also hosted countless major concerts and events. The arena's distinctive red exterior and riverside location made it an iconic part of Detroit's skyline.\n\nThe venue was renowned for its steep seating bowl design that created an intimate atmosphere despite its large capacity. This architectural feature, combined with its excellent sight lines and acoustics, made it a favorite stop for major touring acts. The arena's location along the Detroit River provided a dramatic backdrop for events, while its downtown setting made it easily accessible to metro Detroit audiences.\n\nThough demolished in 2020, 'The Joe' left an indelible mark on Detroit's cultural landscape. During its 38-year run, it hosted numerous memorable concerts from artists like Madonna, The Who, Aerosmith, and Kiss, along with other entertainment events that helped establish Detroit as a major tour stop. While not originally designed primarily for concerts, its versatile configuration and excellent acoustics made it a respected concert venue that played a significant role in Detroit's rich musical heritage.	90	["Wikipedia","Detroit Historical Society","Detroit Free Press archives","NHL.com venue information"]	2025-07-06 21:56:03.555829	completed	[{"event":"Opening Night Concert","date":"1979-12-12","description":"First major event at the arena featuring multiple Detroit-based artists","category":"performance"},{"event":"Demolition","date":"2020","description":"Venue demolished to make way for new development after serving Detroit for 38 years","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Steep Seating Bowl","description":"Unusually steep seating configuration that provided excellent sight lines and created an intimate atmosphere despite large capacity","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Riverside Location","description":"Located along the Detroit River, providing dramatic views and convenient downtown access","category":"location"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Arena was known for having excellent sound quality for concerts despite being primarily designed for sports","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
151	Oracle Park	24 Willie Mays Plaza	San Francisco	CA	94107	US	37.778900	-122.390480	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.002537	f	\N	\N	AT&T Park (now known as Oracle Park) is a waterfront baseball stadium and multi-purpose venue in San Francisco's China Basin neighborhood. Opened in 2000, this architectural marvel combines classic ballpark design with modern amenities, featuring breathtaking views of San Francisco Bay. While primarily serving as the home of the San Francisco Giants, the venue has established itself as a premier concert destination, hosting major artists in a unique setting where the baseball outfield transforms into a massive concert space.\n\nThe venue's most distinctive feature is its waterfront location, where home runs can literally splash into McCovey Cove. For concerts, this creates an extraordinary backdrop with views of the Bay Bridge and city skyline. The stadium's design incorporates San Francisco's maritime heritage with port-hole windows, ship-like lighting standards, and brick and steel construction that echoes the city's industrial past.\n\nAs a concert venue, it offers an intimate atmosphere despite its large capacity, with excellent sight lines and state-of-the-art sound systems specifically configured for musical performances. The venue's location in the heart of San Francisco's tech corridor has influenced its evolution, including multiple corporate naming rights changes and continuous technological upgrades to enhance the audience experience.	90	["MLB.com","San Francisco Giants official website","San Francisco Chronicle archives","Billboard concert database"]	2025-07-06 21:56:08.08623	completed	[{"event":"Paul McCartney Concert","date":"2010-07-10","description":"First major concert held at the venue, setting precedent for future musical events","category":"performance"},{"event":"Opening Day","date":"2000-04-11","description":"First event held at the venue, marking significant architectural achievement in modern ballpark design","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Waterfront Location","description":"Situated directly on San Francisco Bay with views of the Bay Bridge and city skyline","category":"location"},{"feature":"McCovey Cove","description":"Body of water beyond right field that creates unique atmosphere for events","category":"location"},{"feature":"Convertible Field Space","description":"Baseball field converts to concert venue with multiple stage configuration options","category":"stage"}]	f	\N
157	MVP Arena	51 S Pearl St	Albany	NY	12207	US	42.648250	-73.754816	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.008022	f	\N	\N	The Times Union Center (now MVP Arena) is Albany's premier indoor sports and entertainment venue, serving as the Capital Region's largest multi-purpose facility. Originally opened in 1990 as the Knickerbocker Arena, this downtown Albany landmark features a distinctive curved roof design and glass-enclosed atrium entrance. The arena has undergone several renovations and improvements over its history, including a major $19.6 million modernization project completed in 2017 that upgraded the facility's infrastructure and enhanced the fan experience.\n\nThe venue has hosted countless major concerts, sporting events, and family shows, serving as home to the Albany Empire (AFL), Siena Saints basketball, and Albany FireWolves (NLL). Its central location in downtown Albany and versatile configuration options have made it a consistent stop for major touring acts and events. The arena can accommodate up to 17500 spectators for concerts and features modern amenities including luxury suites, club seating, and multiple concession areas.\n\nAs the Capital Region's largest indoor venue, it has played a significant role in Albany's cultural and economic development, drawing over 500000 visitors annually and hosting some of the biggest names in entertainment, from Bruce Springsteen to Billy Joel. The facility's recent modernization and continued investment in improvements demonstrate its ongoing importance to the region's entertainment infrastructure.	90	["Official MVP Arena website","Wikipedia","Albany County website","Times Union newspaper archives","Local historical records"]	2025-07-06 21:57:06.709903	completed	[{"event":"Opening Night Concert - Frank Sinatra","date":"1990-01-30","description":"Frank Sinatra performed the inaugural concert at the newly opened arena","category":"performance"},{"event":"Major Renovation Project","date":"2017","description":"$19.6 million renovation including new exterior facade, upgraded amenities, and modernized infrastructure","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Versatile Configuration","description":"Arena can be configured for various events from concerts to ice hockey to basketball","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Downtown Location","description":"Central location in downtown Albany with easy access to highways and public transportation","category":"location"},{"feature":"Modern Amenities","description":"Features luxury suites, club seating, and recently upgraded concession areas","category":"amenities"}]	f	\N
152	Gasometer	Guglgasse 6	Vienna	9	\N	AT	48.185545	16.418428	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:56:29.987603	f	\N	\N	The Gasometer in Vienna is a remarkable concert and event venue housed within one of four former gas tanks that were part of the city's municipal gasworks from 1896 to 1984. After being decommissioned, these massive brick structures were revitalized in 2001 as part of an innovative urban renewal project. The Gasometer venue occupies Gasometer B, one of the four cylindrical buildings that have been transformed into a mixed-use complex combining residential, commercial, and entertainment spaces.\n\nThe venue is architecturally unique, featuring the original historic brick exterior while the interior has been modernized into a contemporary event space. The cylindrical structure rises approximately 70 meters high and spans 60 meters in diameter, creating an extraordinary acoustic environment and atmospheric setting for concerts. The renovation preserved the industrial character while adding modern amenities and technical infrastructure.\n\nAs a concert venue, the Gasometer has become one of Vienna's most distinctive music venues, hosting both local and international acts across various genres. Its industrial heritage combined with modern functionality makes it a compelling example of adaptive reuse in urban architecture, while its unique cylindrical shape and preserved historical elements create an unparalleled concert atmosphere.	85	["Vienna City Archives","Architectural History Records","Cultural Heritage Documentation","Venue Website"]	2025-07-06 21:56:29.987288	completed	[{"event":"Gasworks Decommissioning","date":"1984","description":"Ceased operation as gas storage facility, marking end of industrial use","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Renovation Completion","date":"2001","description":"Completion of major renovation project converting industrial structure to mixed-use facility including concert venue","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Cylindrical Architecture","description":"Massive brick cylindrical structure approximately 70 meters high and 60 meters in diameter, creating unique acoustic properties and atmospheric setting","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Industrial Heritage","description":"Preserved historical brick exterior maintains industrial character while interior features modern amenities","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Acoustic Properties","description":"Cylindrical shape creates distinctive sound characteristics","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
