185	3Olympia Theatre	72 Dame Street	Dublin	L	\N	IE	53.344180	-6.266080	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.024424	f	\N	\N	The Olympia Theatre is a historic Victorian-era venue located in the heart of Dublin on Dame Street. Originally opened in 1879 as Dan Lowrey's Star of Erin Music Hall, it has been a cornerstone of Dublin's entertainment scene for over 140 years. The venue features a distinctive red brick and stone facade with a classic Victorian theater interior, complete with ornate balconies and plasterwork that has been carefully preserved through various renovations.\n\nThe intimate 1624-seat theater has hosted countless legendary performers and productions throughout its history, from Charlie Chaplin to David Bowie, and has been particularly significant in the development of Irish theater and music. Its perfectly preserved Victorian architecture and excellent acoustics make it one of Dublin's most beloved performance spaces.\n\nIn 2021, the venue entered a new chapter when it was renamed '3Olympia Theatre' following a sponsorship agreement with telecommunications company Three Ireland, though it maintains its historic character and cultural significance. The theater continues to be a vital part of Dublin's cultural landscape, hosting everything from rock concerts to comedy shows while preserving its unique Victorian charm.	90	["Wikipedia","Official Olympia Theatre website","Irish Times archives","Dublin City Council records","Theatre historical society documents"]	2025-07-06 21:59:44.368988	completed	[{"event":"Charlie Chaplin Performance","date":"1910s","description":"Charlie Chaplin performed at the venue during his early career","category":"performance"},{"event":"David Bowie Concert","date":"1969","description":"Notable early performance by David Bowie","category":"performance"},{"event":"Facade Collapse","date":"2004","description":"Parts of the facade collapsed, leading to major restoration work","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Three Ireland Sponsorship","date":"2021","description":"Major sponsorship deal leading to venue renaming","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Victorian Architecture","description":"Preserved Victorian-era theater design with ornate balconies and plasterwork","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Historic Interior","description":"Original Victorian features including ornate ceiling and period details","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Intimate Setting","description":"Relatively small capacity creates intimate atmosphere for performances","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Central Location","description":"Prime location in Dublin's historic center on Dame Street","category":"location"}]	f	\N
160	Sydney Entertainment Centre	35 Harbour St	Sydney	NSW	\N	AU	-33.826345	151.239451	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:57:24.522692	f	\N	\N	The Sydney Entertainment Centre was a major indoor entertainment venue located in Haymarket, Sydney that operated from 1983 to 2015. The venue was purpose-built to host large-scale entertainment events, featuring a distinctive architectural design with its angular roof and glass facade. During its 32-year run, it was Sydney's premier indoor concert arena and hosted many of the world's biggest musical acts.\n\nWith a capacity of approximately 13000, the venue served as Sydney's primary indoor entertainment facility, hosting concerts, sporting events, family shows, and other large-scale productions. The arena featured excellent sight lines and acoustics, making it a preferred venue for both performers and audiences. Its central location near Darling Harbour made it easily accessible and a key part of Sydney's entertainment infrastructure.\n\nThe Centre was demolished in 2015 to make way for the redevelopment of Darling Harbour and was effectively replaced by the ICC Sydney Theatre. During its operational years, it hosted countless memorable performances from artists including Elton John, who performed there over 40 times, making it one of his most-played venues globally.	90	["Wikipedia","Sydney Morning Herald archives","NSW State Archives","Australian Live Music Business Council records"]	2025-07-06 21:57:24.521846	completed	[{"event":"Opening Night with John Denver","date":"1983-05-01","description":"Venue's inaugural performance featuring John Denver","category":"performance"},{"event":"Elton John's Record","date":"1983-2015","description":"Elton John performed over 40 times at the venue, more than any other artist","category":"performance"},{"event":"Final Concert","date":"2015-12-31","description":"Venue's final event before demolition","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Central Location","description":"Prime position in Sydney's Darling Harbour precinct","category":"location"},{"feature":"Angular Roof Design","description":"Distinctive architectural feature that became an iconic part of the Sydney skyline","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Purpose-built design for optimal sound quality for concerts","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
162	Dickey-Stephens Park	400 W Broadway St	North Little Rock	AR	72114	US	34.755862	-92.272880	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:57:34.390944	f	\N	\N	Dickey-Stephens Park is primarily a baseball stadium that serves as the home field for the Arkansas Travelers minor league baseball team. Opened in 2007, this modern ballpark sits along the Arkansas River in North Little Rock, offering spectacular views of the Little Rock skyline. While primarily designed for baseball, the venue occasionally hosts concerts and other special events.\n\nThe park was named after two sets of Arkansas baseball brothers - Bill and George Dickey, and Witt and Jack Stephens. The Stephens family made a significant contribution to the construction of the ballpark. The facility features a natural grass playing surface and modern amenities while maintaining an intimate, classic ballpark atmosphere.\n\nAs a concert venue, Dickey-Stephens Park provides a unique outdoor setting with the capacity to accommodate large crowds in both field and stadium seating configurations. The riverside location and downtown views create a distinctive backdrop for musical performances, though concerts are secondary to its primary function as a baseball facility.	85	["Arkansas Travelers official website","Minor League Baseball database","Local news archives","Municipal records"]	2025-07-06 21:57:34.38913	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"2007-04-12","description":"Official opening of Dickey-Stephens Park as the new home of the Arkansas Travelers","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Riverside Location","description":"Located along the Arkansas River with views of the Little Rock skyline","category":"location"},{"feature":"Natural Grass Field","description":"Features a natural grass playing surface that can be configured for concert events","category":"amenities"},{"feature":"Mixed Seating Options","description":"Combination of fixed stadium seating and potential field s