161	Sidney Myer Music Bowl	Kings Domain Gardens	Melbourne	VIC	3000	AU	-37.823768	144.974628	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:57:35.619401	f	\N	\N	The Sidney Myer Music Bowl is an iconic outdoor amphitheater located in Kings Domain Gardens, Melbourne, featuring a distinctive tensile structure design that was groundbreaking when it opened in 1959. Named after philanthropist Sidney Myer, the venue was gifted to the people of Melbourne by the Sidney Myer Charitable Trust and has since become one of Australia's most beloved outdoor performance spaces. The distinctive canopy design, reminiscent of a butterfly's wings, provides excellent acoustics and shelter for up to 13000 patrons (2000 seated and 11000 on the lawn).\n\nThe venue has hosted countless significant performances and events, from classical concerts by the Melbourne Symphony Orchestra to rock concerts by international superstars. Its most famous event was perhaps the 1967 performance by The Seekers, which drew a record crowd of 200000 people - the largest concert attendance in the Southern Hemisphere at the time. The Bowl underwent a major renovation in 2000-2002 to modernize its facilities while maintaining its architectural integrity.\n\nToday, the Sidney Myer Music Bowl remains a vital part of Melbourne's cultural landscape, hosting everything from summer concerts and festivals to significant community events. Its unique setting in the Kings Domain Gardens, combined with its excellent acoustics and sight lines, makes it one of Australia's premier outdoor performance venues.	90	["Arts Centre Melbourne website","Wikipedia","National Trust of Australia","Victorian Heritage Database","Historical news archives"]	2025-07-06 21:57:35.618343	completed	[{"event":"The Seekers Record-Breaking Concert","date":"1967","description":"Drew 200000 people, largest concert crowd in the Southern Hemisphere at the time","category":"performance"},{"event":"Opening Concert","date":"1959-02-12","description":"Opening performance featuring the Melbourne Symphony Orchestra","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"2000","description":"Extensive renovation and modernization project completed in 2002","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Tensile Structure Design","description":"Distinctive canopy design resembling butterfly wings, one of the earliest examples of tensile architecture in Australia","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Natural Amphitheater Setting","description":"Built into natural slope of Kings Domain Gardens, providing excellent sight lines","category":"location"},{"feature":"Acoustic Design","description":"Canopy structure provides excellent natural acoustics for both amplified and orchestral performances","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
196	VyStar Veterans Memorial Arena	300 A Philip Randolph Blvd	Jacksonville	FL	32202	US	30.326252	-81.645706	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-04-08 06:05:25	2025-07-06 23:22:36.032289	f	\N	\N	The Jacksonville Veterans Memorial Arena (now VyStar Veterans Memorial Arena) is a modern multi-purpose arena that opened in 2003 as part of downtown Jacksonville's Sports Complex. The 875000-square-foot facility replaced the aging Jacksonville Coliseum and was designed specifically to provide superior acoustics and sightlines for concerts and sporting events. Its distinctive architecture features a curved roof design and glass-enclosed grand entrance lobby that has become a landmark in Jacksonville's downtown landscape.\n\nThe arena has established itself as Northeast Florida's premier indoor entertainment venue, hosting major touring artists across all musical genres, family shows, and sporting events. Its versatile design allows for multiple staging configurations and state-of-the-art production capabilities that have attracted top-tier performers and events.\n\nIn 2019, the venue entered a new era when VyStar Credit Union acquired naming rights, though it maintains its tribute to veterans through both its name and military-themed design elements throughout the facility. The arena continues to play a vital role in Jacksonville's cultural landscape while honoring the region's strong military heritage.	90	["Official venue website","City of Jacksonville records","Local news archives","Wikipedia"]	2025-07-06 22:01:07.204987	completed	[{"event":"Arena Opening","date":"2003-11-28","description":"Opened as replacement for old Jacksonville Coliseum with performance by Elton John","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"VyStar Naming Rights Agreement","date":"2019-05-15","description":"Major naming rights agreement with VyStar Credit Union worth $9.76 million over 15 years","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Versatile Configuration","description":"Multiple staging configurations possible for different types of events","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Military Tribute Design","description":"Architectural elements and displays honoring military service members throughout the facility","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Downtown Location","description":"Part of Jacksonville Sports Complex alongside TIAA Bank Field and Baseball Grounds","category":"location"}]	f	\N
165	DAR Constitution Hall	1776 D St NW	Washington	DC	20006	US	38.893966	-77.039999	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:57:49.931973	f	\N	\N	DAR Constitution Hall, located in Washington, DC, is a historic concert venue and auditorium owned and operated by the Daughters of the American Revolution (DAR). Built in 1929, this Neo-Classical building serves as both the organization's headquarters and one of the city's premier performing arts venues. The hall features a stunning four-story auditorium designed by architect John Russell Pope, known for also designing the Jefferson Memorial and National Archives Building.\n\nThe venue has played a significant role in American cultural history, hosting numerous prestigious performances and historic events. Perhaps most notably, it was the site of Marian Anderson's famous 1939 concert controversy, when the DAR refused to let her perform due to her race, leading to her historic Lincoln Memorial concert. Since then, the hall has become a symbol of progress and has hosted countless renowned classical musicians, popular artists, and important political and cultural events.\n\nToday, Constitution Hall remains one of Washington's most active performance venues, known for its superior acoustics and elegant architecture. The auditorium's design provides excellent sightlines and sound quality, making it particularly suitable for classical concerts, though it hosts a wide variety of entertainment events, lectures, and ceremonies.	90	["National Park Service Records","DAR Official Website","National Historic Landmark Database","Library of Congress Archives"]	2025-07-06 21:57:49.931457	completed	[{"event":"Marian Anderson Controversy","date":"1939","description":"DAR's refusal to let African-American singer Marian Anderson perform led to her famous Lincoln Memorial concert","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"National Historic Landmark Designation","date":"1985","description":"Building designated as National Historic Landmark","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Budapest String Quartet Performances","date":"1940s","description":"Regular performances by the renowned classical ensemble","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Neo-Classical Architecture","description":"Designed by John Russell Pope in Neo-Classical style with impressive colonnade facade","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Known for exceptional natural acoustics particularly suited to classical music","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Historic Pipe Organ","description":"Features a historic pipe organ installed in 1929","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
167	SWU Music & Arts	\N	Itu	SP	\N	BR	-23.263900	-47.298900	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:58:05.314703	f	\N	\N	SWU Music & Arts was a music festival venue located in Itu, São Paulo, Brazil that hosted the SWU (Starts With You) Music & Arts Festival in the early 2010s. The festival grounds were specifically developed for large-scale outdoor music events, situated in the interior of São Paulo state. While detailed historical information about the physical venue itself is limited, it served as the location for one of Brazil's notable music festivals that combined musical performances with environmental awareness and sustainability initiatives.	45	["Brazilian music festival databases","News articles about SWU Festival"]	2025-07-06 21:58:05.313743	completed	[{"event":"SWU Music \\u0026 Arts Festival 2010","date":"2010","description":"Inaugural edition of the festival featuring major international and Brazilian artists","category":"performance"},{"event":"SWU Music \\u0026 Arts Festival 2011","date":"2011","description":"Second and final edition of the festival at this location","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Festival Grounds","description":"Large outdoor space capable of hosting major music festival events","category":"location"},{"feature":"Environmental Focus","description":"Venue supported festival's sustainability and environmental awareness mission","category":"amenities"}]	\N	\N
204	Festival d'été de Québec	\N	Québec	QC	\N	CA	46.816667	-71.216667	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-07-16 18:14:59	2025-07-06 22:01:40.473512	f	\N	\N	Festival d'été de Québec (FEQ) is not a single venue but rather a major annual summer music festival that takes place across multiple venues in Quebec City, with its main stage located on the historic Plains of Abraham. Established in 1968, it has grown to become one of North America's largest music festivals, typically running for 11 days in July and featuring both international headliners and local artists.\n\nThe festival's primary venue, the Bell Stage on the Plains of Abraham, is an outdoor concert space that transforms the historic battlefield into a massive natural amphitheater. This main stage area can accommodate up to 80000 spectators, making it one of the largest festival venues in North America. The setting offers a unique combination of historical significance and natural beauty, with the St. Lawrence River and Quebec City's historic architecture as its backdrop.\n\nIn addition to the main stage, the festival utilizes various venues throughout Quebec City, including the Parc de la Francophonie, Imperial Bell theatre, and several indoor and outdoor stages scattered across the city's historic district. This multi-venue approach has helped establish FEQ as a cultural cornerstone of Quebec, bringing together diverse musical genres and attracting hundreds of thousands of visitors annually.	90	["Official Festival Website","Quebec Tourism Board","Historical news archives","Festival annual reports"]	2025-07-06 22:01:40.470717	completed	[{"event":"Festival Founding","date":"1968","description":"Establishment of the festival as a cultural celebration of music and arts in Quebec City","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"The Rolling Stones Performance","date":"2015-07-15","description":"Landmark performance that drew one of the largest crowds in festival history","category":"performance"},{"event":"50th Anniversary","date":"2017","description":"Celebration of 50 years of cultural impact and growth into a major international festival","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Historic Setting","description":"Located on the Plains of Abraham, a historic battlefield and urban park with significant cultural importance","category":"location"},{"feature":"Multi-Venue Format","description":"Festival spans multiple venues across Quebec City, offering diverse performance spaces","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Natural Amphitheater","description":"Main stage utilizes natural terrain of Plains of Abraham to create optimal viewing angles","category":"architecture"}]	\N	\N
164	Palazzo dello Sport	Piazzale Pier Luigi Nervi	Rome	62	00144	IT	41.823984	12.465906	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.013104	f	\N	\N	The PalaLottomatica, located in Rome's EUR district, is one of Italy's largest and most significant indoor sports and entertainment venues. Originally built for the 1960 Summer Olympics as the Palazzo dello Sport, this architectural masterpiece was designed by renowned architect Pier Luigi Nervi in collaboration with Marcello Piacentini. The structure is celebrated for its distinctive dome design and innovative use of concrete, representing a pinnacle of mid-20th century Italian modernist architecture.\n\nThe venue has evolved from its Olympic origins to become Rome's premier indoor arena for major concerts, sporting events, and entertainment spectacles. Its massive concrete dome, spanning 100 meters, creates exceptional acoustics and provides unobstructed views for spectators. The building's design, featuring a circular layout and ribbed dome structure, is considered one of Nervi's greatest achievements and has influenced arena architecture worldwide.\n\nOver the years, the venue has hosted countless significant musical performances, major sporting events, and cultural gatherings. Its transformation through various naming rights agreements, most notably becoming the PalaLottomatica, reflects its ongoing commercial significance while maintaining its architectural integrity and cultural importance to Rome.	85	["Official EUR district documentation","Italian architectural archives","Olympic Games historical records","Italian sports venue databases"]	2025-07-06 21:57:49.933571	completed	[{"event":"1960 Summer Olympics","date":"1960","description":"Hosted basketball events during the Rome Olympics","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Building Construction","date":"1960","description":"Innovative architectural design by Pier Luigi Nervi, featuring revolutionary concrete dome construction","category":"architectural"}]	[{"feature":"Concrete Dome","description":"100-meter spanning concrete dome with distinctive ribbed pattern, representing innovative engineering and architectural design","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Circular Layout","description":"Perfect circular design providing excellent sightlines from all seats","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Natural Acoustics","description":"Dome structure creates unique acoustic properties ideal for concerts","category":"acoustics"}]	f	\N
207	Jerome Schottenstein Center	555 Borror Drive	Columbus	OH	43210	US	40.007589	-83.025030	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-12-01 23:25:04	2025-07-06 22:01:57.386898	f	\N	\N	The Jerome Schottenstein Center, commonly known as 'The Schott,' is a multi-purpose arena located on the campus of The Ohio State University in Columbus, Ohio. Opened in 1998, this impressive 95-million-dollar facility serves as the home court for Ohio State's basketball teams and hosts major concerts and entertainment events. The venue features a distinctive architectural design with a curved roof and glass exterior that has become a landmark on the university campus. With its versatile configuration capabilities, the arena can accommodate various types of events from intimate theater settings to full-arena concerts. The center is named after Jerome Schottenstein, a Columbus businessman and philanthropist whose family made a significant contribution to its construction. Since its opening, it has established itself as one of Central Ohio's premier entertainment venues, hosting numerous high-profile artists and events.	85	["Ohio State University Athletics website","Wikipedia","Columbus Dispatch archives","Venue official website"]	2025-07-06 22:01:57.385806	completed	[{"event":"Venue Opening","date":"1998-11-03","description":"Official opening of the facility with first basketball game","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Flexible Configuration","description":"Arena can be configured for multiple types of events from concerts to sports to theater productions","category":"stage"},{"feature":"Modern Design","description":"Features contemporary architecture with curved roof design and extensive glass exterior","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Location","description":"Situated on Ohio State University campus with excellent accessibility and parking facilities","category":"location"}]	\N	\N
208	Mohegan Sun Arena	1 Mohegan Sun Boulevard	Uncasville	CT	06382	US	41.489590	-72.089130	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-12-05 04:26:30	2025-07-06 22:01:57.436905	f	\N	\N	The Mohegan Sun Arena is a premier entertainment venue located within the Mohegan Sun Casino Resort in Uncasville, Connecticut. Opened in 2001, this modern, multi-purpose arena serves as one of New England's major concert and entertainment destinations. The venue features state-of-the-art acoustics and an intimate seating configuration despite its large capacity, with no seat more than 180 feet from the stage.\n\nThe 10000-seat arena is known for its distinctive design that incorporates elements of Mohegan tribal culture and modern architecture. The facility has hosted numerous high-profile concerts, sporting events, and entertainment shows, establishing itself as a cornerstone of entertainment in the Northeast. The arena is particularly notable for its integration within the larger Mohegan Sun resort complex, allowing concert-goers to enjoy gaming, dining, and hotel amenities in conjunction with events.\n\nSince its opening, the venue has maintained its original name and has become a significant stop on major concert tours, hosting artists ranging from Bruce Springsteen to Lady Gaga. The arena is also home to the WNBA's Connecticut Sun basketball team and has hosted various UFC and boxing events, demonstrating its versatility as an entertainment venue.	90	["Mohegan Sun official website","Wikipedia","Connecticut tourism resources","Local news archives"]	2025-07-06 22:01:57.436729	completed	[{"event":"Arena Opening","date":"2001","description":"Grand opening of the arena as part of Mohegan Sun's major expansion","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Connecticut Sun Debut","date":"2003","description":"Became home court for WNBA's Connecticut Sun, first Native American-owned professional sports team","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Integrated Resort Location","description":"Located within Mohegan Sun Casino Resort, offering seamless access to gaming, dining, and hotel amenities","category":"location"},{"feature":"Intimate Seating Configuration","description":"No seat more than 180 feet from the stage, providing excellent sight lines throughout the venue","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Modern Audio System","description":"State-of-the-art sound system designed specifically for the arena's acoustics","category":"acoustics"}]	\N	\N
209	Salle Pleyel	252 rue du Faubourg Saint-Honor	Paris	IDF	\N	FR	48.849007	2.390791	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2019-03-12 16:49:23	2025-07-06 22:01:57.940069	f	\N	\N	The Salle Pleyel is one of Paris's most prestigious concert venues, originally built in 1927 as both a concert hall and the showroom for Pleyel pianos. Located in the 8th arrondissement, this Art Deco masterpiece was designed by architect Gustave Lion with innovative acoustic engineering by Gustave Lyon. The venue has been a cornerstone of Parisian classical music life for nearly a century.\n\nAfter suffering a devastating fire in 1928, the hall was rebuilt and reopened in 1927, featuring improved acoustics and a striking modernist design. The venue became renowned for its exceptional sound quality and hosted many of the 20th century's greatest classical musicians, including Igor Stravinsky, Maurice Ravel, and Arthur Rubinstein. Its intimate atmosphere and perfect acoustics made it particularly beloved for chamber music and solo recitals.\n\nFollowing extensive renovations between 2002 and 2006, the Salle Pleyel emerged as a modern concert venue while retaining its historical character. Today, it seats 1913 people and continues to host prestigious classical concerts, though it has expanded its programming to include jazz and contemporary music. The venue was acquired by the Cité de la Musique in 2015, marking a new chapter in its storied history.	85	["Wikipedia","Philharmonie de Paris website","Historical archives","French cultural ministry documents"]	2025-07-06 22:01:57.938923	completed	[{"event":"Opening Concert","date":"1927","description":"Inaugural concert featuring works by Beethoven and Ravel","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"2002-2006","description":"Comprehensive modernization while preserving historical features","category":"architectural"},{"event":"Acquisition by Cité de la Musique","date":"2015","description":"Venue integrated into Paris's public music institution network","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Art Deco Architecture","description":"Distinctive Art Deco design by Gustave Lion featuring clean lines and geometric patterns","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Renowned acoustic design by Gustave Lyon, particularly suited for classical music","category":"acoustics"},{"feature":"Historical Piano Connection","description":"Originally built as both concert hall and showroom for Pleyel pianos","category":"historical"}]	\N	\N
168	Estadio Luna Park	Avenida Madero 420	San Nicolás de los Arroyos	B	\N	AR	-34.602249	-58.368628	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 21:58:09.407678	f	\N	\N	Luna Park is one of Argentina's most iconic and historic entertainment venues, located in Buenos Aires (not San Nicolás de los Arroyos as incorrectly listed). Opened in 1932, this legendary arena has been the premier indoor venue in Argentina for boxing, concerts, and cultural events for nearly a century. The venue occupies a distinctive building with Art Deco architectural elements in the heart of Buenos Aires' San Nicolás neighborhood.\n\nOriginally conceived as a boxing stadium, Luna Park has evolved into a versatile entertainment complex that has hosted countless memorable performances by both national and international artists. Its stage has welcomed legendary performers including Frank Sinatra, Luciano Pavarotti, Queen, and numerous Latin American stars. The venue is also historically significant as the location where Eva Perón's body lay in state in 1952.\n\nToday, Luna Park remains Buenos Aires' premier indoor arena, with a capacity of approximately 6000-9000 depending on configuration. The venue continues to host major concerts, sporting events, and cultural gatherings, maintaining its position as a crucial piece of Argentina's entertainment infrastructure.	90	["Wikipedia","Buenos Aires Tourism Board","Historical news archives","Concert databases"]	2025-07-06 21:58:09.407045	completed	[{"event":"Eva Perón lying in state","date":"1952","description":"The venue hosted Eva Perón's body as thousands of Argentinians paid their respects","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Frank Sinatra performance","date":"1981","description":"Legendary performance during Sinatra's South American tour","category":"performance"},{"event":"Queen concert","date":"1981","description":"Part of The Game Tour, one of the most memorable international performances at the venue","category":"performance"}]	[{"feature":"Art Deco architecture","description":"The venue features distinctive Art Deco design elements typical of its 1930s construction","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Central location","description":"Located in the heart of Buenos Aires, making it highly accessible","category":"location"},{"feature":"Versatile configuration","description":"Can be configured for various types of events from concerts to sporting events","category":"stage"}]	\N	\N
174	The Wiltern Theatre	3790 Wilshire Blvd	Los Angeles	CA	90010	US	34.061530	-118.308608	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:28	2025-07-06 23:22:36.016908	f	\N	\N	The Wiltern Theatre is a landmark Art Deco venue located at the corner of Wilshire Boulevard and Western Avenue in Los Angeles. Originally opened in 1931 as a vaudeville theater and movie palace, the building is renowned for its stunning blue-green terra cotta exterior and elaborate Art Deco design elements. The 12-story structure was designed by architect Stiles O. Clements and is considered one of the finest examples of Art Deco architecture in the United States.\n\nAfter facing potential demolition in the 1970s, the venue was saved by preservationists and extensively renovated in the 1980s, transforming it into a premier live performance venue while maintaining its historical character. The interior features an ornate main theater with detailed ceiling medallions, decorative metalwork, and elaborate wall treatments that showcase the height of Art Deco craftsmanship.\n\nToday, the Wiltern is one of Los Angeles's most beloved music venues, hosting major touring acts across all genres while maintaining its architectural integrity as a registered Historic-Cultural Monument. The theater's intimate atmosphere, despite its size, and exceptional acoustics make it a favorite among both performers and concertgoers. Its blend of historical grandeur and modern concert amenities creates a unique entertainment experience that bridges Los Angeles's golden age with contemporary culture.	90	["Los Angeles Conservancy records","National Register of Historic Places documentation","Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument records","Live Nation venue information"]	2025-07-06 21:58:44.258712	completed	[{"event":"Building Construction","date":"1931","description":"Opened as one of the most elaborate Art Deco theaters in Los Angeles","category":"architectural"},{"event":"Historic-Cultural Monument Designation","date":"1973","description":"Designated as Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument #118","category":"historical_event"},{"event":"Major Renovation","date":"1981","description":"Comprehensive renovation and conversion to live performance venue","category":"historical_event"}]	[{"feature":"Art Deco Architecture","description":"Blue-green terra cotta exterior and elaborate Art Deco detailing throughout","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Historic Interior","description":"Original restored architectural details including ceiling medallions and decorative metalwork","category":"architecture"},{"feature":"Superior Acoustics","description":"Theater design provides excellent natural acoustics for live performances","category":"acoustics"}]	f	\N
