13	William Randolph Hearst Greek Theatre	2001 Gayley Rd	Berkeley	CA	94720	US	37.869760	-122.250630	\N	\N	\N	\N	other	active	\N	"[]"	2018-03-24 20:58:27	2025-07-06 23:22:35.910822	f	\N	\N	The William Randolph Hearst Greek Theatre, commonly known as the Greek Theatre or 'the Greek,' is a magnificent 8500-seat amphitheater nestled into the Berkeley hills on the University of California, Berkeley campus. Built in 1903 with funds donated by newspaper magnate William Randolph Hearst, the venue was designed in the style of ancient Greek theaters, specifically modeled after the ancient theatre of Epidaurus. The concrete structure features a classical Greek architectural style with a traditional orchestra level and theatron arrangement.\n\nThe Greek Theatre has been a centerpiece of Berkeley's cultural life for over a century, hosting everything from university graduations to major musical performances. Its stage has welcomed legendary performers including the Grateful Dead, Bob Dylan, Leonard Cohen, and Tom Petty. The venue is particularly renowned for its excellent acoustics, created by its classical bowl shape and hillside setting, as well as its stunning backdrop of the San Francisco Bay and Golden Gate Bridge.\n\nToday, the Greek Theatre remains one of the Bay Area's premier outdoor concert venues, operated in partnership between Another Planet Entertainment and UC Berkeley. The venue typically operates from April through October due to its outdoor nature, and has become famous for its sunset concerts where performances begin in daylight and transition into evening shows under the stars. It was designated as a California Historical Landmark in 1982 and continues to be a vital part of Berkeley's cultural heritage.	90	["UC Berkeley website","California Historical Landmark registry","Wikipedia","Another Planet Entertainment venue information"]	2025-07-06 21:42:43.854321	completed	[{"event":"Venue Construction","date":"1903","description":"Built with donation from William R